// Investment Readiness Assessment: chapters 6 (capacity-building needs) and 7 (enabling environment)
module.exports = [
  { h: 1, t: "Capacity-Building Needs" },
  { p: "This chapter identifies what MSMEs, BSOs and financial institutions need in order to improve investment readiness and make financing more inclusive. The needs follow from the business models, financial practices and readiness ratings in Chapters 3 to 5, and from the enabling-environment evidence in Chapter 7. Sections 6.1 to 6.3 set out the needs that apply across KAZA; Section 6.4 applies them to tourism MSMEs in each Partner State, with the recommended interventions and the support BSOs in each country, and Section 6.5 draws lessons from comparable MSME capacity-building programmes in the region." },
  { p: "The chapter also draws on the needs and capacity survey of SMMEs (small, medium and micro enterprises, the term used for MSMEs in Botswana) and of SMME support institutions that was carried out in Botswana to design the EU-funded Private Sector Development Programme (PSDP), implemented with the Centre for the Development of Enterprise (CDE) (PSDP survey). The survey is more than a decade old, covers Botswana only and spans manufacturing (including agro-industries), tourism, construction and ICT rather than R-NBT alone. Its findings are used here because they closely match the gaps identified in this assessment, which suggests that these needs are persistent rather than new, and because it sets out needs and priorities institution by institution." },
  { h: 2, t: "MSMEs" },
  { b: [
    "**Supplier readiness:** meeting anchor buyers' quality, volume, reliability and price standards; delivery scheduling; packaging and food safety for produce suppliers; managing the supply chain and export quality where products cross borders or are exported (PSDP survey).",
    "**Product development:** moving crafts from generic curios to market-led, storytelling-driven design (MD p. 68); developing décor and furnishing lines to lodge specifications; export packaging, marketing and branding (PSDP survey).",
    "**Quality assurance, standards and productivity:** using quality-assurance schemes and certification to national standards or ISO; on-the-job training so that owners can keep training staff in productivity and quality improvement; workplace productivity methods such as *kaizen* (PSDP survey).",
    "**Aggregation and collective action:** forming and governing cooperatives or producer groups to achieve volume and bargaining power (MD p. 75).",
    "**Financial management:** separating business and household money, simple bookkeeping, budgeting, cost analysis and pricing, cash-flow planning across the season, using mobile money to build a trail.",
    "**Formalisation and compliance:** registration, tax clearance and VAT, insurance, licences and certification, sequenced to buyer and lender requirements. Given the cost and time of formalising (Section 7.1), MSMEs also need practical help through the procedures, not only information about them.",
    "**Contracting and negotiation:** understanding and negotiating supply agreements, payment terms and delivery obligations, which matters more where courts are slow to enforce contracts (Section 7.1); general negotiating skills for dealing with buyers and lenders (PSDP survey).",
    "**Market intelligence and partnerships:** identifying and using opportunities created by regional and international trade agreements; entering and maintaining strategic partnerships with larger and foreign firms; learning to compete with imports and with firms in other countries (PSDP survey).",
    "**Marketing and visibility:** digital listing, trade shows and operator familiarisation (MD p. 75); e-business tools and links to export-oriented web platforms that improve bargaining power (PSDP survey).",
    "**Technical and service skills:** guiding quality (MD p. 84), hospitality, vehicle and boat maintenance, recycling technology.",
    "**Investment readiness:** preparing a business plan, project proposal and loan application; identifying and accessing national, regional and international financing schemes; understanding product terms and negotiating with lenders; and business stewardship, meaning the disciplined use of credit once it is received (PSDP survey).",
    "**Voice and advocacy:** recognising unfair business practices by dominant players, such as late payment or exclusion from procurement, and raising them through associations rather than individually (PSDP survey).",
  ] },
  { h: 3, t: "Evidence from the Botswana PSDP needs and capacity survey" },
  { p: "The PSDP survey ranked SMME capacity-building needs under five types of intervention: technical assistance, mentoring and coaching, on-the-job training, workshops and seminars, and benchmarking or learning visits. Priorities are ranked within each type, with 1 the highest. The table lists every need the survey identified and shows how each applies to the R-NBT value chains." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Prioritised SMME capacity-building needs from the Botswana PSDP survey and their relevance to R-NBT",
    cols: [1500, 3500, 900, 3126],
    head: ["Intervention type", "SMME capacity-building need", "Priority", "Relevance to KAZA R-NBT value chains"],
    rows: [
      ["Technical assistance", "Identify and use market opportunities created by regional and international trade agreements", "1", "Cross-border supply to lodges, including under the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime (Section 7.1); export orders for crafts and nature-positive products"],
      ["Technical assistance", "Advisory services on export value chain management: supply chain management, export quality management and packaging", "2", "Supplier readiness for anchor lodges (quality, volume, packaging); export readiness of nature-positive products"],
      ["Technical assistance", "Sustainable, adapted e-business solutions and market intelligence to improve bargaining power, for example links to export-oriented websites such as BEMA's", "3", "Online visibility and booking for guiding and activities; online sales and curated retail for crafts"],
      ["Technical assistance", "Identify and access regional and international financing schemes", "4", "Readiness grants, grant-to-loan facilities and DFI or donor windows (Section 10.8)"],
      ["Technical assistance", "Identify unfair business practices by dominant players and advocate for their removal", "5", "Payment terms and procurement practices of anchor buyers (Section 7.2); local procurement and reservation rules (Section 7.1)"],
      ["Mentoring and coaching", "Coaching to enter into and maintain regional and international strategic partnerships", "1", "Long-term supply agreements and joint ventures with anchor operators and DMCs, including across borders"],
      ["Mentoring and coaching", "Mentoring on how to compete effectively locally, regionally and internationally", "2", "Lodge-linked goods competing with imports; crafts competing with generic curios"],
      ["Mentoring and coaching", "Coaching on quality-assurance schemes, for example ISO certification", "3", "Food safety and product certification; tourism grading; certification of nature-positive products"],
      ["On-the-job training", "Train SMMEs through on-the-job training and in how to keep training their staff in productivity and quality improvement", "1", "Service quality in guiding and hospitality; consistent finishing in lodge-linked goods; reliable delivery in food supply"],
      ["On-the-job training", "Budgeting and cost analysis; effective operations and manufacturing; how to access development finance (in Botswana, CEDA) and how to manage it", "2", "Costing against imports; seasonal cash-flow planning; use of concessional citizen finance (CEDA, YDF, CEEC, SMEDCO)"],
      ["Workshops and seminars", "Competitiveness, for example export packaging, export marketing and branding", "1", "Crafts and nature-positive products; product storytelling linked to the destination"],
      ["Workshops and seminars", "Export quality and supply chain management; project proposal development to support credit applications; effective negotiating; business stewardship, including the use of business credit, advocacy and lobbying", "2", "Tier 2 and Tier 3 investment readiness: loan applications, negotiation with buyers and lenders, credit discipline"],
      ["Benchmarking and learning visits", "Benchmarking visits to learn regional and international best practice in SMME competitiveness and productivity", "1", "Peer exchange between KAZA destinations and with established supplier and conservancy enterprise programmes"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Technical assistance.** The survey's top priority was to help selected SMMEs identify and use market opportunities created by regional and international trade agreements, which in KAZA applies to cross-border supply to lodges, including under the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime, and to export orders for crafts and nature-positive products. Second came advisory services on export value chain management, covering supply chain management, export quality management and packaging, which correspond to the supplier-readiness needs of anchor lodge suppliers and the export readiness of nature-positive products. Third were sustainable e-business solutions and market intelligence to strengthen bargaining power, for example by linking SMMEs to export-oriented websites such as BEMA's; in R-NBT this means online visibility and booking for guides and activity operators and online and curated sales for crafts. The fourth priority was help to identify and access regional and international financing schemes, which matches the readiness and grant-to-loan facilities in Section 10.8. The fifth was help to identify unfair business practices by dominant players and to advocate for their removal, which in KAZA concerns the payment terms and procurement practices of anchor buyers and the local procurement and reservation rules discussed in Chapter 7." },
      { p: "**Mentoring and coaching.** The survey gave first priority to coaching SMMEs to enter into and maintain regional and international strategic partnerships, which for R-NBT MSMEs means long-term supply agreements and joint ventures with anchor operators and DMCs, including across borders. Second was mentoring on how to compete locally, regionally and internationally, which applies to lodge-linked goods competing with imports and to crafts competing with generic curios. Third was coaching on quality-assurance schemes such as ISO certification, which in KAZA covers food safety, product certification, tourism grading and the certification of nature-positive products." },
      { p: "**On-the-job training.** The first priority was on-the-job training and teaching owners how to keep training their own staff in productivity and quality improvement, which applies to service quality in guiding and hospitality, consistent finishing in lodge-linked goods and reliable delivery in food supply. The second covered budgeting and cost analysis, effective operations and manufacturing, and how to access and manage development finance from CEDA. In the KAZA context these skills underpin costing against imports, seasonal cash-flow planning and the use of concessional citizen finance from CEDA, YDF, CEEC and SMEDCO." },
      { p: "**Workshops and seminars.** The first priority was competitiveness, including export packaging, export marketing and branding, which applies most to crafts and nature-positive products and to product storytelling linked to the destination. The second was a group of topics that correspond to Tier 2 and Tier 3 investment readiness: export quality and supply chain management, project proposal development to support credit applications, effective negotiating, and business stewardship, including the use of business credit, advocacy and lobbying." },
      { p: "**Benchmarking and learning visits.** The survey recommended visits to learn regional and international best practice in SMME competitiveness and productivity. For KAZA, the natural equivalent is peer exchange between destinations and with established supplier and conservancy enterprise programmes." },
    ] },
  { p: "**What the survey adds.** Three points stand out. First, the core needs it identified in Botswana a decade ago (supply chain and quality management, costing, credit applications and access to development finance) are the same as those identified in this assessment, which suggests that general SMME programmes have not closed them. Second, it adds needs that are less prominent in the R-NBT evidence used elsewhere in this assessment: quality assurance and standards, productivity improvement, market intelligence on trade agreements, strategic partnerships, negotiation and collective advocacy, all of which matter for MSMEs that supply anchor lodges or sell across borders. Third, it shows that MSMEs need a mix of delivery modes. The PSDP design concluded that capacity building should go beyond formal training and workshops to include mentoring, exchange visits, technical studies and reviews, coaching and on-the-job training (PSDP survey, Section 3.1). This mix is built into the tiered pathway in Section 10.1." },
  { p: "The priority gaps differ by chain. The main gaps in each priority chain are linked below to the interventions recommended in Chapter 10 and to the partners best placed to deliver them." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "MSME capacity gaps and recommended interventions by value chain",
    cols: [1500, 2600, 2726, 2200],
    head: ["Value chain", "Priority capacity gaps", "Recommended interventions", "Lead delivery partners (indicative)"],
    rows: [
      ["Food and agricultural supply", "Aggregation; consistent quality and volume; food safety and packaging; VAT and tax clearance; cash flow between delivery and payment", "Producer-group formation; irrigation and post-harvest support; lodge supply agreements; contract-backed working capital", "Anchor lodges, LEA, SMEDCO, CEEC, agricultural extension, conservancies"],
      ["Lodge-linked goods", "Design to lodge specifications; consistency and finishing; costing against imports; working capital for materials", "Design and quality coaching with lodge procurement teams; tooling and equipment finance; order-backed working capital", "Anchor operators, NIPDB, LEA, ZimTrade, ZDA, training institutions"],
      ["Crafts and cultural products", "Product differentiation; buyer relationships; pricing; export compliance", "Market-led design; buyer brokerage and curated retail; e-commerce listing; finance only after orders", "Craft hubs, BTO, NTB, ZimTrade, tourism associations"],
      ["Guiding and activities", "Guiding quality and certification; equipment; online visibility; seasonal cash flow", "Guide certification; lease-to-own equipment; digital marketing; seasonal or revenue-linked repayment", "Guides associations, training institutions, DMCs, youth funds"],
      ["Transport and transfers", "Tax clearance; insurance; vehicle maintenance; sub-hire contracts", "Compliance support through one-stop centres; asset finance with seasonal schedules; sub-hire contract templates", "ZIDA, CIPA, ZDA, banks' asset-finance units, DMCs"],
      ["Waste, recycling and cleaning", "Business model; service contracts; equipment; health and safety", "Service-contract brokerage with lodges and towns; equipment grants; youth incubation", "Local authorities, anchor lodges, NGOs, incubators"],
      ["Nature-positive products", "Supply volume; certification; governance; export readiness", "Certification grants; processing equipment; governance support; export order finance", "Conservancies, CBNRM NGOs, CEEC, ZDA, export promotion bodies"],
      ["Accommodation (community)", "Governance; JV negotiation; service standards; tenure", "Governance and JV advisory; grading and service training; blended finance", "BTO, NTB, Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, NACSO, CAMPFIRE Association"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Food and agricultural supply.** Producers need to aggregate, deliver consistent quality and volume, meet food-safety and packaging standards, obtain VAT registration and tax clearance, and bridge the cash-flow gap between delivery and payment. The recommended response is producer-group formation, irrigation and post-harvest support, supply agreements with lodges and contract-backed working capital, delivered by anchor lodges with LEA, SMEDCO, CEEC, agricultural extension services and conservancies." },
      { p: "**Lodge-linked goods.** Makers need to design to lodge specifications, improve consistency and finishing, cost their products against imports and finance materials ahead of delivery. Design and quality coaching with lodge procurement teams, tooling and equipment finance and order-backed working capital address these gaps, with anchor operators, NIPDB, LEA, ZimTrade, ZDA and training institutions as delivery partners." },
      { p: "**Crafts and cultural products.** The gaps are product differentiation, buyer relationships, pricing and export compliance. Market-led design, buyer brokerage and curated retail, and e-commerce listing should come first, with finance offered only once orders exist. Craft hubs, BTO, the Namibia Tourism Board, ZimTrade and tourism associations are the natural partners." },
      { p: "**Guiding and activities.** Guides need quality improvements and certification, equipment, online visibility and help managing seasonal cash flow. Guide certification, lease-to-own equipment, digital marketing support and seasonal or revenue-linked repayment respond to these needs, delivered with guides associations, training institutions, DMCs and youth funds." },
      { p: "**Transport and transfers.** Operators need tax clearance, insurance, vehicle maintenance skills and sub-hire contracts. Compliance support through one-stop centres, asset finance on seasonal schedules and standard sub-hire contract templates are recommended, working with ZIDA, CIPA, ZDA, banks' asset-finance units and DMCs." },
      { p: "**Waste, recycling and cleaning.** Enterprises need a viable business model, service contracts, equipment and health and safety practices. Brokering service contracts with lodges and towns, equipment grants and youth incubation are recommended, delivered with local authorities, anchor lodges, NGOs and incubators." },
      { p: "**Nature-positive products.** The gaps are supply volume, certification, governance and export readiness. Certification grants, processing equipment, governance support and finance against export orders are recommended, with conservancies, CBNRM NGOs, CEEC, ZDA and export promotion bodies as partners." },
      { p: "**Community accommodation.** Community enterprises need stronger governance, better JV negotiation, higher service standards and secure tenure. Governance and JV advisory support, grading and service training and blended finance are recommended, delivered with BTO, the Namibia Tourism Board, the Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, NACSO and the CAMPFIRE Association." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Business Support Organisations" },
  { p: "BSOs in KAZA include public agencies (INAPEM in Angola, LEA in Botswana, NIPDB in Namibia, ZDA and CEEC in Zambia, SMEDCO in Zimbabwe), associations (such as WIBA in Botswana and the tourism associations in each state), conservation NGOs and community institutions. Section 7.3 profiles the main organisations in each country. Their gaps are:" },
  { b: [
    "**Generic rather than sector-specific services.** Most BDS is general business training, not tourism supplier development or R-NBT product development.",
    "**Weak link to finance.** BSOs and financiers operate in silos, producing weak pipelines of investment-ready MSMEs (Financial Landscape Analysis). Few BSOs prepare loan applications or broker introductions to lenders. Where BSOs are also lenders (CEDA, CEEC, SMEDCO), mentoring tends to follow a loan rather than prepare an MSME for one.",
    "**Limited reach in KAZA hubs.** Services are concentrated in capitals; outreach to Kasane, Maun, Katima Mulilo, Rundu, Livingstone, Hwange and Cuando Cubango is thin. INAPEM's first decentralised incubation programme, for example, is in Huíla province rather than Cuando Cubango (Section 7.3).",
    "**Institutional uncertainty.** Some lead agencies are being restructured or are rebuilding systems. Botswana has long-standing plans to rationalise parastatals with overlapping mandates, which unions have questioned (Business Weekly, 2026), and Namibia's BIPA is still procuring a new online registration system (Section 7.1). This makes it harder for BSOs to commit to multi-year supplier-development programmes.",
    "**No diagnostic or tracking tools.** BSOs lack a common readiness diagnostic and do not track MSMEs' progress from tier to tier.",
    "**Gender and youth capacity.** Few BSOs have staff or methods for gender-transformative or youth-specific support.",
    "**Funding.** The 2025 wind-down of USAID programmes removed grant and technical-assistance funding for many community enterprise programmes (Financial Landscape Analysis). Public BSOs also face shrinking government budgets; the PSDP survey already found that Botswana's productivity centre and training authority needed new ways to serve SMMEs and community organisations as public funding declined (PSDP survey).",
    "**Weak coordination and overlapping mandates.** The PSDP design found limited communication and coordination among BDS providers, limited capacity in intermediary organisations, overlapping activities among private-sector associations, and a lack of knowledge, among SMMEs and BSOs alike, of the services each institution offers. It described Botswana's trade support network as broad, with capacity that varied greatly, mandates that often overlapped and weak coordination that led to an ineffective and inefficient use of resources (PSDP survey, Sections 3.1 and 10). The BSO profiles in Section 7.3 suggest that the same holds across KAZA.",
    "**Weak organisational systems.** Several BSOs lack strategic plans, performance management systems and ways to assess and report the impact of their services to members and funders (PSDP survey). Without these, BSOs cannot show which services move MSMEs towards finance.",
  ] },
  { h: 3, t: "Institution-level needs identified in the PSDP survey" },
  { p: "The PSDP survey set out prioritised capacity-building needs for ten Botswana institutions that support SMMEs, including LEA, CEDA and HATAB, which are profiled in Section 7.3. Several institutions have since been renamed or merged: BOCCIM is now Business Botswana, BEDIA was merged into the Botswana Investment and Trade Centre (BITC), and the Ministry of Trade and Industry is now the Ministry of Trade and Entrepreneurship. The needs are summarised below with their implications for R-NBT. Priorities are ranked within each institution, with 1 the highest." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Prioritised capacity-building needs of SMME support institutions in Botswana (PSDP survey) and their implications for R-NBT",
    cols: [1700, 4300, 3026],
    head: ["Institution and role", "Capacity-building needs (priority)", "Implication for KAZA R-NBT"],
    rows: [
      ["**BOCCIM** (now Business Botswana): apex private-sector association", "Capacity to implement the Private Sector Development Strategy (1); sector desks that deal directly with sectors and strengthen priority sectors (1); capacity of the Women in Business Association under BOCCIM to represent its members effectively (1); capacity to act as the apex body for the private sector (1); coaching of staff in research and economic policy analysis on internal and international trade, project management, sector coordination and SMME development (2); a mechanism to assess and report the impact of services to members, with staff trained to use it (2); training of members to engage government and development partners on technical issues (2)", "A tourism or KAZA sector desk could carry MSME supplier issues into public-private dialogue. Strengthening WIBA is a direct route to women-led MSMEs. Impact reporting would support tier tracking (Section 10.1)"],
      ["**BEMA**: exporters' and manufacturers' association", "Technical assistance to build a trade and export database (1); secondment of retired experts to work on specialist projects (2); lobbying and negotiation to reduce the cost of manufacturing and exporting (3); marketing of its services to exporters and manufacturers who are not members (4); an agreement with BOCCIM to transfer exporter and manufacturer members to BEMA, sharing subscription fees (5)", "Export and market data for lodge-linked goods, crafts and nature-positive products. Retired-expert secondments are a low-cost mentoring model"],
      ["**HATAB**: hospitality and tourism industry association", "A strategic plan to deal with its human and financial constraints (1); a performance management system (2)", "HATAB is the association closest to anchor lodges, but its own capacity limits its ability to run supplier development; organisational strengthening should come first"],
      ["**Botswana Training Authority (BOTA)**: training regulator", "Technical expertise to do more for community-based organisations within its budget (1)", "Accredited, affordable training for community trusts and CBO enterprises"],
      ["**National Development Bank (NDB)**: development finance", "A memorandum of understanding with LEA and CDE to strengthen the capacity of its SMME clients (1)", "An early model of the BSO-financier referral agreements recommended in Section 10.5"],
      ["**Ministry of Trade and Industry (MTI)**: policy lead", "Technical expertise to manage the Private Sector Development Strategy (1)", "Policy coordination of MSME support, including tourism supplier development"],
      ["**Botswana Bureau of Standards (BOBS)**: standards and testing", "Strategic alliances with already decentralised organisations so that its services reach the rest of the country without opening offices countrywide (1)", "Standards, testing and certification for suppliers in Kasane and Maun through LEA or other decentralised partners. The survey also noted delays and high costs in quality and standards testing"],
      ["**Botswana National Productivity Centre (BNPC)**: productivity services", "Synergies between the Private Sector Development Strategy and the Economic Diversification Drive in building private-sector capacity (1); renewal in the face of dwindling government funding so that it can keep supporting SMMEs (2); extension of the BNPC-Japanese productivity programme, which carries out *kaizen* and other productivity interventions at company level, to selected SMMEs (3)", "Company-level productivity and quality coaching for lodge suppliers and tourism service enterprises"],
      ["**CEDA**: citizen enterprise finance with mentoring", "Technical expertise to negotiate and deepen its relationship with LEA for the benefit of their shared SMME clients (1); coaching of CEDA's mentors in effective mentoring (2)", "Addresses the gap noted above that mentoring tends to follow a loan rather than prepare an MSME for one; an LEA-CEDA pipeline would link BDS to finance"],
      ["**LEA**: lead public BSO for SMMEs", "A pilot project with CDE on the accreditation (training certification) of its services (1); building the capacity of LEA consultants accredited by BOTA and BOBS (2)", "Accredited supplier-development advisers for the KAZA hubs. LEA is the lead BSO proposed for Botswana (Section 10.5)"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**BOCCIM**, now Business Botswana, is the apex private-sector association. Its first-priority needs were the capacity to implement the Private Sector Development Strategy, sector desks that deal directly with sectors and strengthen priority sectors, a stronger Women in Business Association able to represent its members, and the capacity to act as the apex body for the private sector. Its second-priority needs were coaching for staff in research and economic policy analysis on internal and international trade, project management, sector coordination and SMME development; a mechanism to assess and report the impact of its services to members; and training for members on how to engage government and development partners on technical issues. For R-NBT, a tourism or KAZA sector desk could carry MSME supplier issues into public-private dialogue, strengthening WIBA is a direct route to women-led MSMEs, and impact reporting would support tier tracking." },
      { p: "**BEMA**, the exporters' and manufacturers' association, needed, in order of priority, a trade and export database, the secondment of retired experts to work on specialist projects, support to lobby and negotiate for lower costs of manufacturing and exporting, the marketing of its services to non-members, and an agreement with BOCCIM to transfer exporter and manufacturer members to BEMA with a share of their subscription fees. Its database would serve lodge-linked goods, crafts and nature-positive products, and retired-expert secondments are a low-cost mentoring model. **HATAB**, the hospitality and tourism association, needed a strategic plan to deal with its human and financial constraints and then a performance management system. HATAB is the association closest to anchor lodges, but its own capacity limits its ability to run supplier development, so organisational strengthening should come first." },
      { p: "**The Botswana Training Authority (BOTA)** needed technical expertise to do more for community-based organisations within its budget, which in KAZA means accredited, affordable training for community trusts and CBO enterprises. **The National Development Bank** was to sign a memorandum of understanding with LEA and CDE to strengthen the capacity of its SMME clients, an early model of the BSO-financier referral agreements recommended in Section 10.5. **The Ministry of Trade and Industry**, now the Ministry of Trade and Entrepreneurship, needed technical expertise to manage the Private Sector Development Strategy, including the coordination of MSME support." },
      { p: "**The Botswana Bureau of Standards (BOBS)** needed strategic alliances with already decentralised organisations so that its services could reach the rest of the country without opening offices countrywide; the survey also noted delays and high costs in quality and standards testing. For R-NBT this points to standards, testing and certification for suppliers in Kasane and Maun delivered through LEA or other decentralised partners. **The Botswana National Productivity Centre (BNPC)** needed, in order of priority, synergies between the Private Sector Development Strategy and the Economic Diversification Drive, renewal in the face of dwindling government funding, and the extension of its BNPC-Japanese productivity programme, which applies *kaizen* and other company-level productivity methods, to selected SMMEs. This could provide productivity and quality coaching for lodge suppliers and tourism service enterprises." },
      { p: "**CEDA** needed technical expertise to deepen its relationship with LEA for the benefit of their shared clients and coaching for its mentors in effective mentoring. This addresses the gap that mentoring tends to follow a loan rather than prepare an MSME for one. **LEA** needed a pilot with CDE on the accreditation of its training services and stronger capacity among its consultants accredited by BOTA and BOBS, which would provide accredited supplier-development advisers for the KAZA hubs; LEA is the lead BSO proposed for Botswana." },
    ] },
  { p: "The survey also identified **cross-cutting needs** for all SMME support institutions: training in effective dissemination of information to SMMEs; web-based performance monitoring systems for all support institutions; and, in a joint concept paper by CDE, BOCCIM, BEMA and BEDIA, trade information, trade finance, quality management, trade promotion and national branding, enterprise development, export packaging and institutional strengthening (PSDP survey). These needs apply equally to the BSOs in the other four KAZA states. The PSDP design also valued the participation of CEDA, BEDIA and LEA because their programmes cover the whole country and the whole private sector (PSDP survey, Section 11), which supports this assessment's recommendation to work through existing BSOs with national reach rather than create new ones." },
  { h: 3, t: "Roles of BSOs in building investment readiness" },
  { p: "The PSDP design grouped Botswana's trade support network by function: market promotion and access; quality control, standards and production; trade policy research; technology transfer; financing; and entrepreneurial development (PSDP survey, Section 10). Applied to the readiness pathway in this assessment, BSOs have ten distinct roles. No single BSO can play all of them, so each country needs a lead BSO that coordinates the others and refers MSMEs between them (Section 10.5)." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Roles of BSOs in building MSME investment readiness",
    cols: [1800, 3100, 3026, 1100],
    head: ["Role", "What it involves", "BSOs best placed (examples)", "Tiers served"],
    rows: [
      ["Diagnosis and tracking", "Applying a common readiness diagnostic, placing MSMEs in a tier and tracking their progress", "Lead BSOs: INAPEM, LEA, NIPDB, ZDA, SMEDCO", "0-3"],
      ["Business skills and on-the-job training", "Bookkeeping, budgeting, costing and pricing, operations, productivity and quality, delivered on site as well as in workshops", "Lead BSOs; training institutions; productivity centres such as BNPC; CBNRM NGOs for community enterprises", "0-2"],
      ["Mentoring and coaching", "Accompanying MSMEs after training on quality, competitiveness, partnerships and growth", "CEDA and LEA mentors; retired-expert schemes; NGOs for community enterprises", "1-3"],
      ["Standards, quality and certification", "Testing, product certification, food safety and tourism grading", "Standards bureaux such as BOBS; BTO, NTB and ZTA for grading", "1-2"],
      ["Formalisation support", "Taking MSMEs through registration, tax clearance and licensing when a contract requires it", "CIPA, BIPA, ZIDA, BUE; INAPEM certification", "1-2"],
      ["Market linkage and supplier development", "Buyer brokerage, supplier expos, product development to buyer specifications, export promotion and market information", "Anchor supplier programmes such as Wilderness-LEA; ZimTrade; ZDA; NIPDB; exporters' associations such as BEMA", "1-2"],
      ["Finance readiness and brokerage", "Business plans, proposals and loan packaging; referral to lenders; post-finance monitoring", "LEA-CEDA, ZDA-CEEC, SMEDCO and its Zimbabwe Stock Exchange partnership, NIPDB High Potential Pool", "2-3"],
      ["Representation and advocacy", "Raising MSME issues with government and anchor buyers, such as payment terms, unfair practices and the cost of compliance", "Chambers and apex bodies such as Business Botswana; HATAB, TBCZ, TCZ, FENATA; WIBA; NACSO; CAMPFIRE Association", "All"],
      ["Information and coordination", "Publishing which BSO offers which service; referral between BSOs; monitoring and reporting impact", "Lead BSOs and apex bodies; the KAZA Secretariat at regional level", "All"],
      ["Gender and youth inclusion", "Women's and youth programmes, women's leadership in associations and cooperatives, disaggregated client data", "WIBA; youth and women's finance institutions; the UNDP youth MSME programme in Zambia", "0-2"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Diagnosis and tracking.** Lead BSOs such as INAPEM, LEA, NIPDB, ZDA and SMEDCO should apply a common readiness diagnostic, place MSMEs in a tier and track their progress across all tiers. **Business skills and on-the-job training** in bookkeeping, budgeting, costing and pricing, operations, productivity and quality should be delivered on site as well as in workshops, by lead BSOs, training institutions, productivity centres such as BNPC and, for community enterprises, CBNRM NGOs; this role serves Tiers 0 to 2. **Mentoring and coaching** accompany MSMEs after training on quality, competitiveness, partnerships and growth, through CEDA and LEA mentors, retired-expert schemes and NGOs, and serve Tiers 1 to 3." },
      { p: "**Standards, quality and certification**, covering testing, product certification, food safety and tourism grading, fall to standards bureaux such as BOBS and to grading bodies such as BTO, the Namibia Tourism Board and the Zimbabwe Tourism Authority. **Formalisation support**, which takes MSMEs through registration, tax clearance and licensing when a contract requires it, is the role of CIPA, BIPA, ZIDA, the BUE and INAPEM's certification. **Market linkage and supplier development**, meaning buyer brokerage, supplier expos, product development to buyer specifications, export promotion and market information, is best delivered by anchor supplier programmes such as Wilderness-LEA, ZimTrade, ZDA, NIPDB and exporters' associations such as BEMA. These three roles mainly serve Tiers 1 and 2." },
      { p: "**Finance readiness and brokerage** covers business plans, proposals and loan packaging, referral to lenders and monitoring after finance is received; the partnerships between LEA and CEDA and between ZDA and CEEC, SMEDCO and its stock exchange partnership, and NIPDB's High Potential Pool are the main vehicles, serving Tiers 2 and 3. **Representation and advocacy**, which raises MSME issues such as payment terms, unfair practices and the cost of compliance with government and anchor buyers, is the role of chambers and apex bodies such as Business Botswana, the tourism associations (HATAB, TBCZ, TCZ and FENATA), WIBA, NACSO and the CAMPFIRE Association. **Information and coordination**, meaning publishing which BSO offers which service, referral between BSOs and monitoring impact, belongs to lead BSOs and apex bodies and, at regional level, the KAZA Secretariat. **Gender and youth inclusion** runs through all roles and is led by WIBA, youth and women's finance institutions and programmes such as UNDP's youth MSME programme in Zambia." },
    ] },
  { h: 2, t: "Financial Institutions" },
  { b: [
    "**Seasonal cash-flow lending:** structuring repayment around tourism and agricultural seasons rather than monthly amortisation.",
    "**Sector knowledge:** understanding R-NBT business models, anchor-buyer contracts, concessions and JV arrangements.",
    "**Alternative collateral and data:** using purchase orders, receivables, movable-asset registries, mobile-money data and savings-group records in credit assessment. Zambia's 4th-place ranking for getting credit in Doing Business 2020 shows that the legal tools exist there; Zimbabwe launched a collateral registry for movable assets in 2022, and Botswana's CIPA is developing a National Collateral Registry for SME credit. The gap is lenders' use of these tools for small borrowers. In Angola, which ranked 185th for getting credit, lenders have few such tools and need contractual workarounds such as payment routing through the anchor buyer.",
    "**Small-ticket economics:** reducing the unit cost of small loans through digital origination, group or value-chain lending, and BSO pre-screening.",
    "**Guarantee use:** many banks under-use existing credit guarantee schemes; staff need to understand when and how to use them.",
    "**Gender-smart lending:** collecting sex-disaggregated portfolio data, designing products for women-owned enterprises and training staff to reduce bias.",
    "**Regenerative screening:** applying simple environmental and social criteria consistent with a KAZA R-NBT eligibility framework.",
  ] },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Summary of capacity gaps by actor and priority",
    cols: [1900, 3626, 1300, 2200],
    head: ["Actor", "Priority capacity gaps", "Priority", "Chains most affected"],
    rows: [
      ["MSMEs (producers, node A)", "Aggregation, quality, basic records, savings discipline", "High", "Food, crafts, nature-positive"],
      ["MSMEs (suppliers and services, nodes C-D)", "Reliability, quality assurance and standards, compliance, contracting and negotiation, cash-flow planning, investment readiness", "High", "Lodge-linked goods, food, transport, guiding"],
      ["Cooperatives and community enterprises (nodes B, E)", "Governance, benefit-sharing, certification, leadership including women and youth", "High", "Nature-positive, crafts, accommodation"],
      ["BSOs", "Sector-specific supplier development; readiness diagnostic; finance brokerage; KAZA outreach; standards and productivity services outside capitals; coordination and information on services; strategic planning, performance and impact monitoring; mentor accreditation; gender and youth methods", "High", "All"],
      ["Financial institutions", "Seasonal structuring; alternative collateral; guarantee use; gender-disaggregated data", "Medium-High", "All, especially supply chains"],
      ["Anchor buyers", "Supplier-development commitment; payment terms that support MSME cash flow; contract templates", "Medium", "Food, lodge-linked goods, transport"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**In summary,** four groups have high-priority capacity gaps. Producers at node A need aggregation, better quality, basic records and savings discipline, above all in food, crafts and nature-positive products. Suppliers and service enterprises at nodes C and D need reliability, quality assurance and standards, compliance, contracting and negotiating skills, cash-flow planning and investment readiness, particularly in lodge-linked goods, food, transport and guiding. Cooperatives and community enterprises at nodes B and E need governance, benefit-sharing rules, certification and leadership that includes women and youth, especially in nature-positive products, crafts and accommodation. BSOs, across all chains, need sector-specific supplier development, a readiness diagnostic, finance brokerage, outreach in the KAZA hubs, standards and productivity services outside the capitals, better coordination and information on who offers which service, organisational systems for strategic planning and for monitoring performance and impact, accredited mentors and advisers, and gender and youth methods." },
      { p: "Financial institutions' gaps are of medium to high priority and affect all chains, especially supply chains: seasonal structuring, alternative collateral, guarantee use and gender-disaggregated data. Anchor buyers' gaps are of medium priority but matter for food, lodge-linked goods and transport: a commitment to supplier development, payment terms that support MSME cash flow, and standard contract templates." },
    ] },

  { h: 2, t: "Tourism MSME Needs, Interventions and Support BSOs by Country" },
  { p: "The needs above apply across KAZA, but their weight, the interventions that fit and the BSOs able to deliver them differ by Partner State. This section applies them to tourism MSMEs in the KAZA area of each country: enterprises that sell to tourists directly (guiding, activities, transfers, accommodation, crafts) and those that supply lodges and operators (food, lodge-linked goods, services, nature-positive products). For each country it sets out the main needs, the recommended intervention and its delivery mode (using the PSDP survey's types: technical assistance, mentoring and coaching, on-the-job training, workshops and benchmarking visits), the support BSOs, with the proposed lead BSO first (Section 10.5), and a priority rating." },
  { p: "The ratings are the author's assessment from the evidence in Chapters 2 to 5 and 7 and the Financial Landscape Analysis. Evidence is uneven: Botswana, Zimbabwe and Zambia are better covered than Namibia (7 of 71 survey responses) and Angola (no survey responses) (MD p. 10, 109), so the Namibian and Angolan tables in particular should be tested at the national validation workshops." },

  { h: 3, t: "Angola: Cuando and Cubango" },
  { p: "Tourism in Angola's KAZA component is nascent. Existing tourism MSMEs are guesthouses, restaurants and transport businesses in Menongue and Cuito Cuanavale, with emerging interest in river expeditions, birding, fishing and cultural and heritage tourism (Financial Landscape Analysis). Access is limited by poor roads, landmine contamination and sparse services, and bookings, guiding and logistics for Angola's flagship Luiana camp currently accrue in Namibia (MD p. 25, 118-119). Most enterprises are in Tiers 0 and 1. Banks ask for INAPEM certification before they lend (AllAfrica, 2026), and foreign shareholding in road passenger transport is capped at 49% (MD p. 26). The approach therefore has to be readiness first." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Angola: tourism MSME capacity-building needs, interventions and support BSOs",
    cols: [1400, 2400, 2350, 1876, 1000],
    head: ["Need area", "Specific need in the KAZA area", "Recommended intervention (delivery mode)", "Support BSOs (lead first)", "Priority"],
    rows: [
      ["Formalisation and certification", "Registration, INAPEM certification (needed for bank finance and tax benefits) and MINTUR licensing and classification of guesthouses and restaurants, none of which is easy to obtain from Menongue", "Registration and certification clinics in Menongue and Cuito Cuanavale, with follow-up to completion (technical assistance)", "INAPEM; BUE and Guiché Único; MINTUR; provincial governments of Cuando and Cubango", "High"],
      ["Basic business and financial management", "Bookkeeping, separating business and household money, costing and pricing, managing a seasonal income", "A tourism module in INAPEM's incubator network delivered in the province, with on-site coaching (on-the-job training, workshops)", "INAPEM and the Envolver+ incubator network; Universidade Cuito Cuanavale", "High"],
      ["Guiding, expedition and logistics skills", "Angolan guides, boat operators and logistics providers able to serve Luiana and the river systems, so that this business is no longer captured in Namibia", "Guide and boat-operator training and certification; apprenticeships with operators on the Namibian side and with expedition partners (on-the-job training, benchmarking visits)", "MINTUR; Universidade Cuito Cuanavale; National Geographic Okavango Wilderness Project; Namibian operators and training institutions", "High"],
      ["Hospitality service standards", "Service, hygiene and food-safety standards in guesthouses and restaurants that meet classification and visitor expectations", "Hospitality service training in the workplace, linked to MINTUR classification (on-the-job training, coaching)", "MINTUR; hospitality associations (AHORESIA); Universidade Cuito Cuanavale", "Medium"],
      ["Product development and market linkage", "Turning birding, fishing, river, cultural and heritage experiences into bookable products and linking them to operators and DMCs", "Product development support and familiarisation trips with operators; learning visits to Namibian and Botswana destinations (technical assistance, benchmarking visits)", "MINTUR; provincial governments; conservation NGOs; AIPEX for investor partners", "Medium"],
      ["Community enterprise", "Governance and benefit-sharing for community associations and cooperatives that want to host or supply tourism", "Group formation and governance support, with small grants (technical assistance, mentoring)", "ACADIR and local NGOs; The HALO Trust (local employment and access); traditional authorities", "Medium"],
      ["Investment readiness and finance", "Business plans for FGC-guaranteed loans and micro-loans; savings-group linkage", "Business-plan support once enterprises are certified and trading; links to KixiCrédito and FGC windows (mentoring)", "INAPEM; KixiCrédito and Banco Sol; FGC", "Low (after readiness)"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Formalisation and certification** is a high priority. Tourism MSMEs in Cuando and Cubango need to register, obtain INAPEM certification, which banks ask for and which gives access to tax benefits, and obtain MINTUR licensing and classification for guesthouses and restaurants, none of which is easy from Menongue. Registration and certification clinics in Menongue and Cuito Cuanavale, with follow-up until each enterprise has completed the process, should be run by INAPEM with the BUE and Guiché Único, MINTUR and the provincial governments. **Basic business and financial management** is equally urgent: bookkeeping, separating business and household money, costing and pricing, and managing a seasonal income. A tourism module in INAPEM's incubator network, delivered in the province with on-site coaching, would meet this need, working with the Envolver+ network and Universidade Cuito Cuanavale." },
      { p: "**Guiding, expedition and logistics skills** are the third high priority, so that Angolan guides, boat operators and logistics providers can serve Luiana and the river systems and this business is no longer captured in Namibia. Training and certification, apprenticeships with operators on the Namibian side and with expedition partners, delivered with MINTUR, Universidade Cuito Cuanavale, the National Geographic Okavango Wilderness Project and Namibian operators and training institutions, are recommended. Of medium priority are **hospitality service standards** in guesthouses and restaurants, through workplace training linked to MINTUR classification with the hospitality associations and the university; **product development and market linkage**, turning birding, fishing, river, cultural and heritage experiences into bookable products through familiarisation trips and learning visits to Namibian and Botswana destinations; and **community enterprise** governance and benefit-sharing, supported by ACADIR, local NGOs, The HALO Trust and traditional authorities. **Investment readiness** for FGC-guaranteed loans and micro-loans from KixiCrédito and Banco Sol should follow once enterprises are certified and trading." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Support BSO gaps in Angola.** No tourism-specific BDS was identified in the KAZA area, INAPEM's decentralised incubation started in Huíla rather than Cuando Cubango, and the hospitality associations are Luanda-based (Section 7.3). The first need is a BSO presence in Menongue: an INAPEM desk or incubator node with a tourism module, working with MINTUR and the provincial governments." },

  { h: 3, t: "Botswana: Chobe District and Ngamiland" },
  { p: "Botswana's KAZA component centres on Kasane and Maun, with high-value, low-volume tourism in the Okavango Delta, Chobe, Linyanti and Makgadikgadi. It has the strongest anchor-led procurement evidence in KAZA. Wilderness buys more than 80% of its supplies by value from Botswana-based suppliers, its supplier expo generated more than BWP 6.2 million of new contracts for citizen-owned businesses in its first year, and its suppliers can borrow from Stanbic Bank against their contracts (Section 7.2). Guesthouses and touristic transfer companies are reserved for citizens, which makes them MSME space that depends on domestic capital and lodge contracts (MD p. 32-34). Camp construction materials still come largely through South African intermediaries (MD p. 68), and a Maun craft hub connecting more than 100 artisans has export certifications but no committed international buyers (MD p. 69). Most tourism MSMEs are in Tiers 1 and 2. The PSDP survey (Section 6.1) is Botswana evidence and applies directly." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Botswana: tourism MSME capacity-building needs, interventions and support BSOs",
    cols: [1400, 2400, 2350, 1876, 1000],
    head: ["Need area", "Specific need in the KAZA area", "Recommended intervention (delivery mode)", "Support BSOs (lead first)", "Priority"],
    rows: [
      ["Supplier readiness for anchor camps", "Meeting camp requirements for food, dry goods, beverages, building materials, laundry, maintenance and transfers in Kasane and Maun, and replacing South African intermediaries", "Supplier development tied to named anchors' procurement plans, with supplier expos and nominated suppliers (technical assistance, mentoring)", "LEA; Wilderness and other anchor operators; HATAB; WIBA", "High"],
      ["Investment readiness and contract finance", "Loan packaging, using contracts as security, credit discipline after a loan", "Contract-backed finance on the Wilderness-Stanbic model; LEA-CEDA referral pipeline; coaching of CEDA mentors (mentoring, workshops)", "LEA; CEDA; Stanbic Bank Botswana and other banks; YDF for young entrepreneurs", "High"],
      ["Quality, standards and grading", "Product testing and certification for food and goods suppliers; grading for citizen guesthouses; affordable testing outside Gaborone", "Standards and grading services delivered in Kasane and Maun through LEA; quality-assurance coaching (mentoring and coaching)", "LEA; BOBS; BTO", "High"],
      ["Crafts and cultural products", "Moving from generic curios to differentiated, market-led products; finding committed buyers; export packaging and branding", "Market-led design with lodge shops and buyers; buyer brokerage and curated retail; export packaging and branding workshops (technical assistance, workshops)", "BTO; craft hubs; WIBA; BITC for export buyers", "Medium"],
      ["Guiding and mokoro services", "Guiding quality and certification; equipment; online visibility for small activity operators", "Guide certification and refresher training; lease-to-own equipment; digital listing (on-the-job training)", "BTO; Botswana Guides Association; mokoro polers' trusts", "Medium"],
      ["Business management and productivity", "Costing against imports, budgeting, seasonal cash-flow planning, workplace productivity", "On-site productivity and quality coaching for suppliers, including BNPC company-level methods (on-the-job training)", "LEA; BNPC", "Medium"],
      ["Community trust enterprises", "Governance, JV negotiation and enterprise development in community trusts, such as the Eastern Panhandle's conservation-compatible products", "Governance and JV advisory; certification and equipment grants for nature-positive products (technical assistance)", "BOCOBONET; CBNRM NGOs; BTO", "Medium"],
      ["Voice and payment terms", "Raising payment terms, procurement access and the cost of compliance with anchors and government", "A tourism desk in the apex business body; supplier representation in HATAB dialogue with members (technical assistance to associations)", "Business Botswana; HATAB; WIBA", "Low-Medium"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "Three needs are of high priority in Botswana. **Supplier readiness for anchor camps** means meeting camp requirements for food, dry goods, beverages, building materials, laundry, maintenance and transfers in Kasane and Maun, and replacing South African intermediaries. Supplier development tied to named anchors' procurement plans, with supplier expos and nominated suppliers, should be led by LEA with Wilderness and other anchor operators, HATAB and WIBA. **Investment readiness and contract finance** covers loan packaging, using contracts as security and credit discipline after a loan. The Wilderness-Stanbic model of contract-backed finance should be extended to other anchors and banks, with an LEA-CEDA referral pipeline, coaching for CEDA's mentors and YDF for young entrepreneurs. **Quality, standards and grading** covers product testing and certification for food and goods suppliers and grading for citizen guesthouses; LEA, BOBS and BTO should deliver these services in Kasane and Maun rather than only in Gaborone." },
      { p: "Of medium priority are **crafts and cultural products**, where the need is to move from generic curios to differentiated products with committed buyers, supported by BTO, the craft hubs, WIBA and BITC through market-led design, buyer brokerage and export packaging and branding; **guiding and mokoro services**, through certification, lease-to-own equipment and digital listing with BTO, the Botswana Guides Association and the mokoro polers' trusts; **business management and productivity**, through on-site coaching by LEA and BNPC; and **community trust enterprises**, through governance, JV advisory and certification grants with BOCOBONET, CBNRM NGOs and BTO. **Voice and payment terms** are of lower priority but matter for suppliers: a tourism desk in Business Botswana and supplier representation in HATAB's dialogue with its members would carry these issues to anchors and government." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Support BSO gaps in Botswana.** LEA is the natural lead, but needs tourism supplier-development staff in Kasane and Maun and accredited consultants. HATAB needs the strategic plan and performance system the PSDP survey identified before it can host supplier development. BOBS and BNPC need delivery partners in the KAZA hubs, and LEA and CEDA need a working referral agreement (Section 6.2)." },

  { h: 3, t: "Namibia: Zambezi and Kavango East" },
  { p: "Namibia's KAZA component spans the Zambezi Region and Kavango East, with Katima Mulilo, Rundu, Divundu and Kongola as hubs and a dense network of conservancies. The gaps are clearest in lodge-linked goods: about 80% of lodge design and décor items in the Kavango and Zambezi regions are imported because local suppliers cannot match imports on consistency, quality, scale and price (MD p. 70, 128). Community SMMEs capture little value: craft, soap and oil, textile, village-tour and taxi enterprises together earn under 1% of conservancy returns, against 59.7% from joint-venture lodges (MD p. 37, 40). Company registration is slow (Section 7.1), and the end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025 has shifted demand (Section 7.2). Gondwana's sourcing at its Etosha lodge shows that local suppliers can meet lodge specifications when sourcing is designed in from the start (Section 7.2)." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Namibia: tourism MSME capacity-building needs, interventions and support BSOs",
    cols: [1400, 2400, 2350, 1876, 1000],
    head: ["Need area", "Specific need in the KAZA area", "Recommended intervention (delivery mode)", "Support BSOs (lead first)", "Priority"],
    rows: [
      ["Lodge-linked goods to specification", "Décor, furnishings, building materials and gifts that match imports on consistency, quality, scale and price", "Design and quality coaching with lodge procurement teams; product specifications agreed in advance; tooling finance (mentoring and coaching, technical assistance)", "NIPDB; NCCI Zambezi and Kavango branches; Kavango river lodges and other operators", "High"],
      ["From conservancy benefit to enterprise", "Turning conservancy crafts, soap and oil, textiles, village tours and taxis into commercial enterprises that sell to lodges", "Conservancy enterprise-to-supplier pathway: governance, business skills and buyer links (technical assistance, mentoring)", "NIPDB with NACSO, IRDNC and NNF; conservancies", "High"],
      ["Formalisation", "Completing company registration despite delays at BIPA, and staying compliant to avoid de-registration", "Registration help at the point of need, through NCCI regional branches (technical assistance)", "NIPDB; BIPA; NCCI regional branches", "High"],
      ["Market diversification", "Finding several buyers and regional and domestic markets as international demand shifts", "Product inclusion in DMC itineraries; multi-lodge supply; marketing support (technical assistance, workshops)", "NTB; FENATA, HAN and TASA; DMCs", "Medium"],
      ["Nature-positive products", "Supply volume, certification and governance in honey, beekeeping and forest products", "Certification and equipment grants; producer-group support (technical assistance)", "NNF; IRDNC; EIF and CCFN", "Medium"],
      ["Guiding and hospitality skills", "Guiding, hospitality and enterprise management skills in the regions; NTB registration and grading", "Skills-to-enterprise tracks in guiding and hospitality (on-the-job training)", "Namibia Training Authority; UNAM Katima Mulilo campus; NUST hospitality school; NTB", "Medium"],
      ["Investment readiness", "Pitches, business plans and grant and loan applications for youth- and women-owned businesses and cooperatives", "Pitch coaching and investment readiness support linked to the SME fund, the High Potential Pool, DBN and the CGS (mentoring, workshops)", "NIPDB; DBN; CGS partner banks", "Medium"],
      ["Waste and recycling services", "Viable business models and service contracts for community recycling and cleaning enterprises", "Service-contract brokerage with lodges and towns; equipment grants (technical assistance)", "Regional councils; lodge operators; NGOs", "Low"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "Three needs are of high priority in Namibia. **Lodge-linked goods to specification** means décor, furnishings, building materials and gifts that match imports on consistency, quality, scale and price. Design and quality coaching with lodge procurement teams, specifications agreed in advance and tooling finance should be delivered by NIPDB with the chamber's Zambezi and Kavango branches and lodge operators. **Moving from conservancy benefit to enterprise** means turning conservancy crafts, soap and oil, textiles, village tours and taxis into commercial enterprises that sell to lodges, through a pathway of governance, business skills and buyer links run by NIPDB with NACSO, IRDNC, NNF and the conservancies. **Formalisation** needs practical help with registration at BIPA, and with staying compliant to avoid de-registration, through the chamber's regional branches." },
      { p: "Of medium priority are **market diversification**, through product inclusion in DMC itineraries, supply to several lodges and marketing support with NTB, FENATA, HAN, TASA and DMCs; **nature-positive products**, through certification and equipment grants and producer-group support with NNF, IRDNC, EIF and CCFN; **guiding and hospitality skills**, through skills-to-enterprise tracks with the Namibia Training Authority, UNAM's Katima Mulilo campus, the NUST hospitality school and NTB; and **investment readiness**, through pitch coaching linked to the SME fund, the High Potential Pool, DBN and the credit guarantee scheme. **Waste and recycling services**, supported by regional councils, lodges and NGOs, are a lower priority with a smaller evidence base." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Support BSO gaps in Namibia.** NIPDB's programmes are national and not tourism-specific, so a KAZA supplier-development service has to be built with the conservancy support organisations and lodge operators (Section 7.3). The chamber's regional branches are the most practical local delivery point, but need advisers trained in tourism supply and registration support." },

  { h: 3, t: "Zambia: Livingstone and the southern KAZA area" },
  { p: "Zambia's KAZA tourism MSMEs are concentrated in Livingstone and along the Victoria Falls-Kazungula corridor, with community enterprise around Simalaha and Sioma Ngwezi. Linkages with hotels are better documented here than in most destinations: a Livingstone hotel buys produce directly from smallholders, and a women's mushroom farm sells most of its produce to hotels under long-term contracts (Section 7.2). A women's forest-products cooperative sells mungongo oil and baobab coffee to Livingstone hotels and export buyers but cannot finance a ZMW 30,000 certification quote (MD p. 73). Licensing is the main regulatory burden, and omnibus licensing for accommodation is under discussion (Section 7.1). Women lead more than 60% of Zambia's MSMEs (AFI), and youth BDS is emerging through UNDP's youth-led MSME programme in Livingstone (Section 7.3)." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Zambia: tourism MSME capacity-building needs, interventions and support BSOs",
    cols: [1400, 2400, 2350, 1876, 1000],
    head: ["Need area", "Specific need in the KAZA area", "Recommended intervention (delivery mode)", "Support BSOs (lead first)", "Priority"],
    rows: [
      ["Smallholder aggregation and hotel supply", "Moving smallholders from individual spot sales to organised, consistent supply under agreements with Livingstone hotels", "Producer-group formation; supply agreements; post-harvest and quality coaching (technical assistance, mentoring)", "ZDA and CEEC; Livingstone Tourism Association; hotels", "High"],
      ["Certification and export readiness", "Product certification, quality assurance and export compliance for nature-positive products such as forest products", "Certification grants; quality-assurance coaching; COMESA and regional market linkage (mentoring and coaching, technical assistance)", "ZDA; CEEC; Peace Parks Foundation", "High"],
      ["Youth enterprise in tourism services", "Guiding, transfers, digital marketing and services enterprises for young people", "Destination incubation building on the UNDP youth programme; apprenticeships with operators (on-the-job training, mentoring)", "ZDA; UNDP and BongoHive; Hotel and Tourism Training Institute; Livingstone tourism university", "High"],
      ["Licensing and compliance", "Obtaining and renewing the several licences tourism MSMEs need; road service licence rules for transport", "Licensing help at the point of need; advocacy for omnibus licensing (technical assistance, advocacy)", "BRRA; Ministry of Tourism; ZDA; TCZ and ZCSMBA for advocacy", "Medium"],
      ["Guiding and hospitality service quality", "Guiding quality, a shared bottleneck in the corridor, and hospitality service standards", "Guide certification and workplace hospitality training (on-the-job training)", "Hotel and Tourism Training Institute; Livingstone tourism university; tour operator and safari associations", "Medium"],
      ["Women-led enterprise finance", "Records and digital payment trails that lenders recognise; access to finance for women-led enterprises", "Bookkeeping and mobile-money training; links to women's finance windows and the ZCGS (workshops, mentoring)", "CEEC; ZCSMBA; partner banks and MFIs", "Medium"],
      ["Investment readiness and seasonal finance", "Business plans and loan applications; repayment matched to the tourism season", "Investment readiness coaching; seasonal terms in CEEC products (mentoring)", "CEEC; ZDA; ZCGS", "Medium"],
      ["Community enterprise", "Governance and enterprise development in community conservancy areas", "Governance support and enterprise grants (technical assistance)", "Peace Parks Foundation; traditional authorities", "Medium"],
      ["Cross-border small trade", "Using the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime to supply across the Zambia-Zimbabwe border, especially for women traders", "Awareness and documentation support at Victoria Falls and Kazungula (workshops)", "ZDA; ZCSMBA; women traders' groups", "Low"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "Three needs are of high priority in Zambia. **Smallholder aggregation and hotel supply** means moving smallholders from individual spot sales to organised, consistent supply under agreements with Livingstone hotels, through producer-group formation, supply agreements and post-harvest and quality coaching led by ZDA and CEEC with the Livingstone Tourism Association and hotels. **Certification and export readiness** for nature-positive products such as forest products needs certification grants, quality-assurance coaching and links to COMESA and regional markets, delivered by ZDA, CEEC and Peace Parks Foundation. **Youth enterprise in tourism services**, in guiding, transfers, digital marketing and services, should build on the UNDP youth programme in Livingstone, with ZDA, BongoHive, the Hotel and Tourism Training Institute and the Livingstone tourism university providing incubation and apprenticeships." },
      { p: "Of medium priority are **licensing and compliance**, through help at the point of need from the BRRA, the Ministry of Tourism and ZDA, with TCZ and ZCSMBA advocating omnibus licensing; **guiding and hospitality service quality**, through certification and workplace training with the training institutions and tour operator associations; **women-led enterprise finance**, through bookkeeping and mobile-money training and links to women's finance windows and the ZCGS, with CEEC, ZCSMBA and partner lenders; **investment readiness and seasonal finance**, through coaching and seasonal terms in CEEC products; and **community enterprise** in conservancy areas, with Peace Parks Foundation and traditional authorities. **Cross-border small trade** under the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime is a lower but specific need for women traders at Victoria Falls and Kazungula." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Support BSO gaps in Zambia.** The ZDA-CEEC agreement combines BDS with finance, but is national rather than tourism-specific, and CEEC products are not yet designed around the tourism season. Tourism associations in Livingstone represent operators but rarely provide supplier development (Section 7.3). A Livingstone-based supplier-development service, run by ZDA and CEEC with the Livingstone Tourism Association, would close this gap." },

  { h: 3, t: "Zimbabwe: Victoria Falls, Hwange and Binga" },
  { p: "Zimbabwe's KAZA tourism MSMEs are concentrated around Victoria Falls, Hwange and Binga and in the CAMPFIRE districts. Anchor demand is clear: a Hwange anchor lodge calls irrigated fruit and vegetable supply the clearest near-term opportunity because sourcing from Bulawayo brings delays and quality problems, and new suppliers need only two or three successful bookings to be listed regularly (MD p. 75). Informality carries a heavy penalty: an anchor operator reports a 30% withholding tax on payments to suppliers without tax clearance (MD p. 74). Only about one in seven MSMEs is registered (FinScope MSME Zimbabwe 2022), only 17% always keep records, and women own more than 60% of MSMEs, most of them informal (Chapters 4 and 5). Passenger transport and local arts and crafts are reserved for citizens (Section 7.1), and currency instability complicates lending (Section 7.4)." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Zimbabwe: tourism MSME capacity-building needs, interventions and support BSOs",
    cols: [1400, 2400, 2350, 1876, 1000],
    head: ["Need area", "Specific need in the KAZA area", "Recommended intervention (delivery mode)", "Support BSOs (lead first)", "Priority"],
    rows: [
      ["Tax clearance and formalisation", "Registration and tax clearance so that suppliers avoid the 30% withholding tax and can be contracted by anchors", "Tax-clearance and registration support at the point of need, through the ZIDA one-stop centre and ZIMRA (technical assistance)", "SMEDCO; ZIDA; ZIMRA; Victoria Falls City Council and RDCs for local licences", "High"],
      ["Irrigated produce supply to lodges", "Irrigation, aggregation, consistent quality and supply agreements for Hwange and Victoria Falls lodges", "Producer-group formation; irrigation and post-harvest support; supply agreements with lodges (technical assistance, mentoring)", "SMEDCO; anchor lodges; Lupane State University and agricultural extension", "High"],
      ["Records and financial management", "Basic records, costing and cash-flow management, including in foreign currency", "Bookkeeping and mobile-money training with on-site follow-up (on-the-job training, workshops)", "SMEDCO; Ministry of Women Affairs, Community, Small and Medium Enterprises Development", "High"],
      ["Supplier listing and marketing", "Securing the first bookings and orders that lead to regular listing; costs of marketing and trade shows", "Sub-contracts and apprenticeships with anchor operators; joint marketing (mentoring, on-the-job training)", "TBCZ; ZTA; ZimTrade; DMCs", "Medium"],
      ["Guiding standards", "Guiding quality and certification for small activity operators", "Guide training and certification (on-the-job training)", "Zimbabwe Professional Hunters and Guides Association; Safari Operators Association of Zimbabwe; ZTA", "Medium"],
      ["Crafts and CAMPFIRE community enterprise", "Differentiated crafts and community enterprises in CAMPFIRE districts that sell to lodges and visitors", "Market-led design and buyer links; governance support for community enterprises (technical assistance)", "CAMPFIRE Association; Hwange, Binga and Tsholotsho RDCs; ZimTrade for export buyers", "Medium"],
      ["Women-led enterprises", "Formalisation, records and finance for women-owned MSMEs, most of which are informal", "Women's group-to-enterprise pathway; links to women's finance (mentoring, workshops)", "Ministry of Women Affairs, Community, Small and Medium Enterprises Development; Zimbabwe Women's Microfinance Bank", "Medium"],
      ["Investment readiness and finance", "Loan applications, governance for growth enterprises, and managing currency risk", "Investment readiness support, including for ZEEX; links to ECGC-guaranteed lending and EmpowerBank for young entrepreneurs (mentoring)", "SMEDCO; ECGC; EmpowerBank", "Medium"],
      ["Cross-border small trade", "Using the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime within Zimbabwe's lower limit", "Awareness and documentation support at Victoria Falls (workshops)", "ZimTrade; ZIMRA", "Low"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "Three needs are of high priority in Zimbabwe. **Tax clearance and formalisation** comes first, so that suppliers avoid the 30% withholding tax and can be contracted by anchors; SMEDCO should take enterprises through the ZIDA one-stop centre and ZIMRA, with the Victoria Falls City Council and the RDCs for local licences. **Irrigated produce supply to lodges** in Hwange and Victoria Falls needs irrigation, aggregation, consistent quality and supply agreements, supported by SMEDCO, anchor lodges, Lupane State University and agricultural extension. **Records and financial management**, including cash-flow management in foreign currency, needs bookkeeping and mobile-money training with on-site follow-up from SMEDCO and the Ministry of Women Affairs, Community, Small and Medium Enterprises Development." },
      { p: "Of medium priority are **supplier listing and marketing**, through sub-contracts, apprenticeships and joint marketing with TBCZ, ZTA, ZimTrade and DMCs; **guiding standards**, through certification with the guides' and safari operators' associations and ZTA; **crafts and CAMPFIRE community enterprise**, through market-led design, buyer links and governance support with the CAMPFIRE Association, the RDCs and ZimTrade; **women-led enterprises**, through a group-to-enterprise pathway and links to the Zimbabwe Women's Microfinance Bank; and **investment readiness and finance**, through SMEDCO's support, including for ZEEX, ECGC-guaranteed lending and EmpowerBank. **Cross-border small trade** under the Simplified Trade Regime is a lower priority, supported by ZimTrade and ZIMRA." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Support BSO gaps in Zimbabwe.** SMEDCO combines lending with capacity building, but its presence in Victoria Falls and Hwange is limited, and its mentoring, like that of other lender-BSOs, tends to follow a loan (Section 6.2). The tourism associations represent operators but do not run supplier development. A supplier-development service for Victoria Falls and Hwange, run by SMEDCO with ZIDA, TBCZ and the CAMPFIRE Association, is the main gap." },

  { h: 3, t: "Comparison across the five countries" },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Priority of tourism MSME capacity-building needs by country (author's assessment)",
    cols: [2626, 1280, 1280, 1280, 1280, 1280],
    head: ["Need area", "Angola", "Botswana", "Namibia", "Zambia", "Zimbabwe"],
    rows: [
      ["Formalisation, licensing and tax clearance", "High", "Low", "High", "Medium", "High"],
      ["Business and financial management", "High", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium", "High"],
      ["Supplier readiness and aggregation for lodges", "Low", "High", "High", "High", "High"],
      ["Quality, standards, grading and certification", "Medium", "High", "Medium", "High", "Medium"],
      ["Guiding and hospitality skills", "High", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium"],
      ["Product development and marketing", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium"],
      ["Community enterprise and governance", "Medium", "Medium", "High", "Medium", "Medium"],
      ["Investment readiness and finance", "Low", "High", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium"],
      ["Women and youth enterprise", "Medium", "Medium", "Medium", "High", "Medium"],
      ["Proposed lead BSO", "INAPEM", "LEA", "NIPDB", "ZDA and CEEC", "SMEDCO"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Comparing the five countries,** formalisation, licensing and tax clearance are a high priority in Angola, Namibia and Zimbabwe, where certification, slow registration and the withholding tax respectively stand between MSMEs and anchor contracts, but a lower priority in Botswana, where registration is online and the binding needs are supplier readiness, standards and contract finance. Supplier readiness and aggregation for lodges is a high priority everywhere except Angola, where the tourism market is too young and the priorities are basic business management, formalisation and guiding skills. Quality, standards and certification are most pressing in Botswana and Zambia, community enterprise in Namibia's conservancies, investment readiness in Botswana, where contract finance is already available, and women and youth enterprise in Zambia, where women lead most MSMEs and a youth programme is already running in Livingstone. The proposed lead BSOs are INAPEM in Angola, LEA in Botswana, NIPDB in Namibia, ZDA and CEEC in Zambia and SMEDCO in Zimbabwe." },
    ] },

  { h: 2, t: "Lessons from Comparable Programmes" },
  { p: "The PSDP survey is one of several MSME capacity-building programmes in or near the KAZA states whose experience can inform this programme. This section summarises others that deal with supplier development, community and natural-product enterprises, tourism SMEs, entrepreneurship training, women's enterprise and MSME finance, together with South Africa's Tourism Enterprise Partnership, the closest tourism-specific precedent in the region. Programmes already discussed elsewhere in this report (Envolver and Envolver+ in Angola, the Wilderness supplier programme and its links with LEA and Stanbic in Botswana, NIPDB's High Potential Pool and SME fund in Namibia, and UNDP's youth-led MSME programme in Zambia) are not repeated." },
  { note: "Source caveat. The evaluation reports and programme pages behind this section could not be opened for this draft. The details below come from search summaries of those sources and are marked \"to verify\" in the References. Several results are reported by the programmes or their partners rather than by independent evaluators, and are labelled as such. Figures should be checked against the original documents before the report is finalised." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Comparable MSME capacity-building programmes and lessons for KAZA tourism MSMEs",
    cols: [1000, 1700, 2200, 2000, 2126],
    head: ["Country", "Programme", "What it did", "Reported results (type of evidence)", "Lesson for KAZA tourism MSMEs"],
    rows: [
      ["Botswana", "UNDP Business Supplier Development Programme (from 2018)", "Three-way model: large companies as buyers, citizen-owned SMEs as suppliers and experienced Batswana consultants as mentors; sectors were mining, agro-processing, infrastructure projects, leather and textiles", "Consultants mentored each SME for about ten months to meet buyers' quality standards; participating SMMEs reported about P300 million in profit (programme- and press-reported)", "Apply the three-way model with lodges and DMCs as buyers and LEA or accredited consultants as mentors; secure buyer commitment before enrolling suppliers"],
      ["Namibia", "Millennium Challenge Account Namibia, Tourism Project (2009-2014, USD 68 million)", "Etosha management and infrastructure, tourism marketing and capacity building for communal conservancies; ten eco-tourism projects worth N$17 million handed over to nine Zambezi conservancies", "Tourism rose slightly, but the increase could not be attributed to the project; infrastructure proved more sustainable than marketing and institutional reform; no control group for the conservancy support evaluation (independent evaluation)", "Plan follow-on funding for marketing and institutional support; set a baseline and a comparison group from the start (Annex B)"],
      ["Namibia", "Millennium Challenge Account Namibia, Indigenous Natural Products activity (USD 7.6 million)", "Quality, volume and sales of five native plant products harvested mainly by women; training of producer and processor organisations; an innovation fund; market information", "The sector became more organised; 60 producer and processor organisations were trained but their staff found the content too complex; better quality raised sales for one product but not the others (independent evaluation)", "Pitch training at the level of community producer groups; choose products by buyer demand; coordinate harvesters, processors and buyers together"],
      ["Namibia", "SME Bank (state-backed lender to small businesses)", "Deposit-taking bank for SMEs, majority-owned by government", "Placed under Bank of Namibia control in 2017 and later liquidated after risky investments, including about N$200 million placed with South African entities; small businesses lost access to their operating funds (press reports)", "Route MSME finance through well-governed, regulated lenders with guarantees; keep BSO roles separate from deposit-taking and lending"],
      ["Zambia", "World Bank Zambia Agribusiness and Trade Project (from 2016, USD 40 million); second phase (USD 170 million)", "Market linkages and firm growth in agribusiness, including matching grants; the second phase includes matching grants for producer organisations", "Outcome rated satisfactory, monitoring and evaluation modest; lessons: matching grants depend on investment in selecting and coaching beneficiaries, mentoring and tailored support matter, intermediaries can reach small firms (World Bank completion report, 2024)", "Pair any grant-to-loan facility with careful selection and coaching; explore co-financing of lodge food-supply groups with the second phase"],
      ["Zambia", "World Bank Green, Resilient and Transformational Tourism Development Project (2023, USD 100 million)", "Matching grants for communities in the nature-based economy, infrastructure including Livingstone town centre works, and sector governance", "Under implementation; MSME eligibility not confirmed (World Bank press release)", "Coordinate so that community matching grants and this programme's readiness support reinforce each other in the Zambian KAZA area"],
      ["Zambia", "ITC SheTrades Zambia (from 2019)", "Training, mentoring and market connections for women-owned businesses", "200 women trained and mentored; more than USD 3 million in new sales and 126 new jobs in 18 months; a second phase followed (partner-reported)", "Combine export readiness with buyer introductions for women-led enterprises such as the forest-products cooperative (Section 6.4)"],
      ["Namibia and region", "ITC ACCESS! for African businesswomen in international trade", "Trade support for women exporters; the programme the PSDP proposed for Botswana", "More than 2,600 businesswomen reached in 19 countries since 2005; no Namibian results found (ITC)", "A ready model for women's export readiness in crafts and nature-positive products"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "Zimbabwe Destination Development Programme, Victoria Falls (supported by IFC)", "SME marketing training, mentoring and licensing reform, with the tourism ministry, ZTA and TBCZ; SME needs analysis", "Dates and results not confirmed (press report)", "Build on existing Victoria Falls work with ZTA and TBCZ rather than start a parallel programme"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "UN Tourism ST-EP project on youth and women in tourism at Victoria Falls (2017)", "Hospitality training, business advice and small subsidies for young and women entrepreneurs", "More than 100 participants, over half of them women (UN Tourism)", "Combine skills training with business advice and small start-up support for youth and women (Section 10.3)"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "ILO Start and Improve Your Business, delivered through the BG4YE youth employment project", "Business training, with trainers developed among BDS providers, for example in Bulawayo", "More than 1,017 businesses improved output and 80% expanded into new or related lines (ILO-reported, 2025)", "Train and certify local trainers in BSOs so that support continues after the programme"],
      ["Angola", "UNCTAD Empretec (EU-funded, 2018-2021)", "Behavioural entrepreneurship training workshops", "About 360 entrepreneurs trained, 37% of an early cohort women; four Angolans certified as national trainers, two as master trainers (UNCTAD)", "Certify national trainers who can deliver in Menongue; combine with INAPEM's incubator network"],
      ["South Africa (regional benchmark)", "Tourism Enterprise Partnership and the Western Cape Tourism Mentorship Programme (2010-2014)", "Tourism-specific SMME linkage support; mentoring pairs of established tourism firms and SMMEs for six months", "Mentoring addressed gaps that training alone left; research found that large firms doubt small firms' capacity and procurement habits restrict access, and that generic SMME support overlooks tourism needs (academic studies)", "Keep supplier development tourism-specific, work with anchors' procurement teams, and use firm-to-firm mentoring"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Botswana.** UNDP's Business Supplier Development Programme, started at the end of 2018, uses a three-way model in which large companies act as buyers, citizen-owned SMEs as suppliers and experienced Batswana consultants as mentors, in mining, agro-processing, infrastructure projects, leather and textiles. Consultants mentored each SME for about ten months to meet buyers' quality standards, and participating SMMEs have reported about P300 million in profit, a figure reported by the programme and the press rather than by an evaluator. UNDP credits the buyers' willingness to buy locally as essential. For KAZA, the same model could be applied with lodges and DMCs as buyers and LEA or accredited consultants as mentors, with buyer commitment secured before suppliers are enrolled." },
      { p: "**Namibia.** The Millennium Challenge Account's USD 68 million Tourism Project (2009-2014) improved Etosha's management and infrastructure, marketed Namibian tourism and built the capacity of communal conservancies; ten eco-tourism projects worth N$17 million were handed over to nine Zambezi conservancies. The independent evaluation found that tourism rose slightly but could not attribute the rise to the project, and that infrastructure proved more sustainable than marketing and institutional reform; the conservancy support evaluation had no control group. The lessons are to plan follow-on funding for marketing and institutional support and to set a baseline and comparison group from the start. The USD 7.6 million Indigenous Natural Products activity worked on the quality, volume and sales of five native plant products harvested mainly by women. The sector became more organised, but staff of the 60 producer and processor organisations trained found the content too complex, and better quality raised sales for only one product. Training for community producer groups must be pitched at their level, and products chosen by buyer demand. Namibia's state-backed SME Bank, placed under central bank control in 2017 and later liquidated after risky investments, left small businesses without access to their operating funds; MSME finance should go through well-governed, regulated lenders, with BSO roles kept separate from deposit-taking and lending." },
      { p: "**Zambia.** The World Bank's Zambia Agribusiness and Trade Project used matching grants to build market linkages; its 2024 completion report rated the outcome satisfactory but monitoring and evaluation modest, and concluded that matching grants depend on investment in selecting and coaching beneficiaries, that mentoring and tailored support matter and that intermediaries can reach small firms. Its second phase offers matching grants to producer organisations and could co-finance lodge food-supply groups. The World Bank's 2023 tourism project provides matching grants to communities in the nature-based economy, which this programme's readiness support should complement. ITC's SheTrades Zambia trained and mentored 200 women and reported more than USD 3 million in new sales and 126 new jobs in 18 months, showing the value of combining export readiness with buyer introductions for women-led enterprises. ITC's ACCESS! programme, which the PSDP proposed for Botswana, has reached more than 2,600 businesswomen in 19 countries and is a ready model for women exporters of crafts and nature-positive products." },
      { p: "**Zimbabwe.** An IFC-supported destination programme in Victoria Falls offered SME marketing training, mentoring and licensing reform with the tourism ministry, ZTA and TBCZ; its dates and results could not be confirmed, but it is existing work to build on. A UN Tourism project in 2017 combined hospitality training, business advice and small subsidies for more than 100 young and women participants, over half of them women. The ILO reports that more than 1,017 businesses supported under its BG4YE youth employment project, which used Start and Improve Your Business training delivered through local BDS providers, improved their output, and that 80% expanded. Training and certifying local trainers keeps support going after a programme ends." },
      { p: "**Angola and the regional benchmark.** UNCTAD's EU-funded Empretec programme trained about 360 Angolan entrepreneurs between 2018 and 2021 and certified four national trainers, two of them as master trainers, a model for delivering training in Menongue alongside INAPEM's incubator network. In South Africa, the Tourism Enterprise Partnership and the Western Cape Tourism Mentorship Programme paired established tourism firms with SMMEs for six months. Research found that mentoring filled gaps that training alone left, that large firms doubt small firms' capacity and their procurement habits restrict access, and that generic SMME support overlooks the needs of tourism businesses. Supplier development in KAZA should therefore be tourism-specific, work with anchors' procurement teams and use firm-to-firm mentoring." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Cross-cutting lessons and where they apply.** Seven lessons recur across these programmes:" },
  { b: [
    "**Anchor on committed buyers.** Supplier development works when buyers commit to buy before suppliers are trained (UNDP SDP, Tourism Enterprise Partnership, Wilderness). This underpins the anchor supplier package (Section 10.4).",
    "**Mentor over months, not days.** Six- to ten-month mentoring (UNDP SDP, Tourism Mentorship Programme) achieved what training alone did not, which supports the mix of delivery modes in the tiered pathway (Section 10.1).",
    "**Pitch support at the right level.** Training that was too complex for community producer organisations limited results (Indigenous Natural Products), so Tier 0 and Tier 1 content for community enterprises should be simple and practical.",
    "**Select and coach grant recipients.** Matching grants deliver only when recipients are carefully selected and coached (Zambia Agribusiness and Trade Project), which applies to the grant-to-loan facility (Section 10.8).",
    "**Build local delivery capacity.** Certifying national and local trainers (Empretec, Start and Improve Your Business) keeps support going after a programme ends and fits the recommendation to accredit BSO mentors and advisers (Section 10.5).",
    "**Fund institutions beyond the project and measure from the start.** Marketing and institutional gains faded faster than infrastructure, and results could not be attributed without a baseline (Millennium Challenge Account Tourism Project), which supports the disaggregated baseline in Annex B and performance-based BSO contracts (Section 10.5).",
    "**Keep finance in sound, regulated institutions.** The failure of Namibia's SME Bank shows the risk of mixing development mandates with weak governance in deposit-taking lenders; this programme should work through regulated lenders and guarantee schemes (Section 10.6).",
  ] },

  { h: 1, t: "Enabling Environment" },
  { p: "This chapter evaluates the conditions outside the enterprise that shape investment readiness: national and regional regulation, market access and links with larger tourism operators, business development services, and wider policy conditions. Section 7.1 draws on the World Bank's Doing Business 2020 country reports to compare the five states. Section 7.2 adds evidence on market access from sources outside the Macro Diagnostic, and Section 7.3 profiles the main BSOs in each country." },

  { h: 2, t: "National and Regional Regulatory Frameworks" },
  { h: 3, t: "What the Doing Business country reports show" },
  { p: "The World Bank's Doing Business 2020 economy profiles are the most recent source that measures business regulation in all five KAZA states in the same way. They score ten areas of regulation that a small or medium-sized domestic limited company meets over its life, from starting a business to resolving insolvency, and rank 190 economies. The data were collected to May 2019 and describe the largest business city in each country (Luanda, Gaborone, Windhoek, Lusaka and Harare), not the KAZA hubs, where procedures are usually slower and offices further away. The series was discontinued in 2021, so the rankings are best read as a comparison between the five states and a guide to which procedures weigh most on MSMEs." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Doing Business 2020: overall and topic rankings of the five KAZA states (out of 190 economies)",
    cols: [2626, 1280, 1280, 1280, 1280, 1280],
    head: ["Indicator", "Angola", "Botswana", "Namibia", "Zambia", "Zimbabwe"],
    rows: [
      ["**Ease of doing business: rank (score out of 100)**", "**177 (41.3)**", "**87 (66.2)**", "**104 (61.4)**", "**85 (66.9)**", "**140 (54.5)**"],
      ["Starting a business", "146", "159", "165", "117", "167"],
      ["Dealing with construction permits", "120", "44", "84", "67", "140"],
      ["Getting electricity", "156", "139", "76", "129", "167"],
      ["Registering property", "167", "82", "173", "149", "109"],
      ["Getting credit", "185", "80", "80", "4", "67"],
      ["Protecting minority investors", "147", "72", "88", "72", "97"],
      ["Paying taxes", "106", "59", "n.a. (score 74.5)", "17", "146"],
      ["Trading across borders", "174", "55", "138", "155", "159"],
      ["Enforcing contracts", "186", "137", "64", "130", "169"],
      ["Resolving insolvency", "168", "84", "127", "79", "142"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Overall rankings.** Of 190 economies, Zambia ranked 85th (score 66.9 out of 100) and Botswana 87th (66.2), the two easiest places to do business among the KAZA states. Namibia followed at 104th (61.4), Zimbabwe at 140th (54.5) and Angola at 177th (41.3)." },
      { p: "**Topic rankings by country.** **Angola** ranked 146th for starting a business, 120th for construction permits, 156th for getting electricity, 167th for registering property, 185th for getting credit, 147th for protecting minority investors, 106th for paying taxes, 174th for trading across borders, 186th for enforcing contracts and 168th for resolving insolvency. **Botswana** ranked 159th for starting a business, 44th for construction permits, 139th for electricity, 82nd for property, 80th for credit, 72nd for minority investors, 59th for taxes, 55th for trading across borders, 137th for enforcing contracts and 84th for insolvency. **Namibia** ranked 165th for starting a business, 84th for construction permits, 76th for electricity, 173rd for property, 80th for credit, 88th for minority investors, 138th for trading across borders, 64th for enforcing contracts and 127th for insolvency; its paying-taxes score was 74.5, but its rank could not be confirmed. **Zambia** ranked 117th for starting a business, 67th for construction permits, 129th for electricity, 149th for property, 4th for credit, 72nd for minority investors, 17th for taxes, 155th for trading across borders, 130th for enforcing contracts and 79th for insolvency. **Zimbabwe** ranked 167th for starting a business, 140th for construction permits, 167th for electricity, 109th for property, 67th for credit, 97th for minority investors, 146th for taxes, 159th for trading across borders, 169th for enforcing contracts and 142nd for insolvency." },
    ] },
  { p: "Source: World Bank, Doing Business 2020 report and economy profiles. Topic ranks were transcribed from the published profiles; the overall ranks and scores are confirmed in the World Bank's published rankings." },
  { p: "Five findings stand out for R-NBT MSMEs:" },
  { b: [
    "**Formalising is slow everywhere.** All five states ranked 117th or lower for starting a business. Registering a company took about 8.5 days in Zambia, 27 days and 9 procedures in Zimbabwe, 36 days and 8 procedures in Angola, and 54 days and 10 procedures in Namibia. In Botswana in 2020, company registration could be completed online in a day, but tax registration took about 30 days; later US government reports put tax registration at about a week (US Department of State, 2020 and 2026). For a micro-enterprise earning most of its income in a five-month season, a registration process that takes one to two months has a real opportunity cost. This supports treating formalisation as a milestone tied to a contract (Section 4.3) and offering hands-on help with procedures.",
    "**Credit infrastructure varies widely.** Zambia ranked 4th in the world for getting credit (score 95 of 100), reflecting strong legal rights for borrowers and lenders over movable assets and good credit-information coverage. Zimbabwe (67th), Botswana and Namibia (both 80th) were mid-ranked, while Angola ranked 185th (score 5 of 100). Where the legal framework is strong, receivables and movable-asset lending to suppliers is legally feasible and the constraint is lender practice; in Angola, finance will depend for some time on grants, guarantees and contractual arrangements with anchor buyers.",
    "**Contracts are hard to enforce.** Enforcing a commercial contract through the courts took about 660 days in Botswana (137th) and 410 days at a cost of 83% of the claim in Zimbabwe (169th); Angola ranked 186th and Zambia 130th. Only Namibia (64th) ranked in the top half. Contract-backed supplier finance in KAZA therefore cannot rely on court enforcement. It needs arrangements in which the anchor buyer pays the lender directly, as in the Wilderness-Stanbic facility, where suppliers cede their right to receive payment from Wilderness to the bank (Mmegi, 2025).",
    "**Tax compliance burdens differ.** Zambia ranked 17th for paying taxes and Botswana 59th, but Zimbabwe ranked 146th, and Namibia's tax system required 27 payments and 302 hours a year for the standard firm. Combined with the 30% withholding tax on suppliers without tax clearance in Zimbabwe (MD p. 74), this explains why tax clearance is the formalisation step that matters most to anchor buyers.",
    "**Cross-border trade is costly.** Four of the five states ranked 138th or lower for trading across borders; only Botswana (55th) performed well. This matters in a transfrontier area where Hwange lodges, Kasane suppliers and Livingstone hotels could source across borders. Small traders face border procedures designed for larger consignments (see the regional frameworks below).",
  ] },
  { p: "Two further indicators affect specific chains. Getting electricity was slow and costly in Zimbabwe (167th), Angola (156th), Botswana (139th) and Zambia (129th), which raises the cost of processing, cold storage and workshop production for food, nature-positive products and lodge-linked goods, and strengthens the case for solar equipment finance. Registering property was difficult in Namibia (173rd), Angola (167th) and Zambia (149th); although most R-NBT MSMEs operate on communal land that cannot be mortgaged in any case, the rankings show that even freehold property is a slow route to collateral." },
  { h: 3, t: "Country profiles" },
  { p: "**Angola** (177th) had the weakest business environment of the five, with bottom-ten rankings for getting credit and enforcing contracts. Government has tried to simplify entry: Presidential Decree 40/12 created the Balcão Único do Empreendedor (BUE) in 2012 to simplify the formation and licensing of micro and small enterprises and bring informal activity into the formal economy, and by 2021 a new platform at the Guiché Único da Empresa allowed a company to be created online or in person in about an hour when documents were in order (Ver Angola, 2021). INAPEM certification gives access to tax benefits, and banks ask for the certificate when a company applies for finance (AllAfrica, 2026). Sector rules cap foreign shareholding at 49% in road passenger transport and 50% in road freight (MD p. 26). For Cuando Cubango, the binding constraints are a thin enterprise base, distance from Luanda-based services, and the fact that guiding and logistics for Angolan sites accrue in Namibia (MD p. 118-119)." },
  { p: "**Botswana** (87th) combined good rankings for construction permits (44th), trading across borders (55th) and paying taxes (59th) with weak rankings for starting a business (159th), getting electricity (139th) and enforcing contracts (137th). The Companies and Intellectual Property Authority (CIPA) runs an online business registration system, which the Ministry of Trade and Entrepreneurship credits with faster turnaround times. Its 2026/27 budget speech also reported that the World Bank's B-READY 2025 report recognised Botswana's strong legal frameworks and CIPA's digital front door, but flagged insolvency, tax compliance, dispute resolution and market competition as areas needing attention; it announced a home-grown company registration system and a National Collateral Registry to improve SME access to credit (Ministry of Trade and Entrepreneurship, 2026). Sector rules are the most protective of citizen MSMEs in the region: fresh-produce and general-dealer licences, guesthouses and touristic transfer companies are reserved for citizens (MD p. 31-34), Botswana law reserves 35 service sectors for citizens (US International Trade Administration), and the Economic Inclusion Act 2021, in force since April 2022, provides for the participation of targeted citizens in the economy, including through procurement and licensing. These rules create protected demand for citizen suppliers but close processing, distribution and transport to outside equity." },
  { p: "**Namibia** (104th) ranked well for getting electricity (76th) and enforcing contracts (64th), the best of the five states, but poorly for starting a business (165th) and registering property (173rd). Formalisation remains the weak point. In June 2024 the Business and Intellectual Property Authority (BIPA) said it was procuring an integrated online registration system expected within 18 to 24 months (The Brief, 2024); in May 2025 the responsible minister said the existing online system was not working (The Namibian, 2025); and in August 2025 BIPA announced that it would de-register about 1,000 non-compliant companies a month (The Brief, 2025). Namibia was among the new economies covered by the B-READY 2025 report (Ecofin, 2026). In tourism, Namibia has a mature conservancy JV framework but low MSME value capture (MD p. 37, 40), and the end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025 has affected demand from Europe (Section 7.2)." },
  { p: "**Zambia** (85th) had the best overall ranking and strong scores for getting credit (4th) and paying taxes (17th), but weak ones for trading across borders (155th), registering property (149th) and enforcing contracts (130th). Licensing is the main recurring burden: more than a quarter of Zambian firms cite business licensing and permits as a major obstacle, nearly double the regional average, and Zambia is moving to risk-based regulation with World Bank support (World Bank, 2024). After a 2025 benchmarking mission to Mauritius, the Business Regulatory Review Agency (BRRA) began talks with the Ministry of Tourism and the Zambia Tourism Agency on adopting omnibus licensing, in which one licence covers several activities, for the accommodation sub-sector (Zambia Monitor, 2025). Road service licences require at least 25% Zambian membership and all-Zambian directors unless the applicant holds a ZDA licence (MD p. 44), which favours local transport MSMEs." },
  { p: "**Zimbabwe** (140th) rose 15 places in Doing Business 2020 and was among the World Bank's top 20 improvers, with reforms in five areas; improvements in starting a business, including lower business licensing fees in Harare, were the main driver (World Bank, 2019; The Herald). It still ranked low for starting a business (167th), getting electricity (167th), paying taxes (146th) and enforcing contracts (169th). The Zimbabwe Investment and Development Agency (ZIDA) runs a One Stop Investment Services Centre, and in January 2024 ZIDA and ZIMRA signed a memorandum of understanding to integrate their electronic and investor services (ZIMRA, 2024). Ownership rules have tightened. In 2018 the indigenisation law was narrowed to a list of sectors reserved for citizens that included passenger transport and the provision, marketing and distribution of local arts and crafts (Veritas, 2018). Statutory Instrument 215 of 2025 then set new rules for foreign participation in reserved sectors; press reports describe 14 reserved sectors and a requirement for foreign-owned businesses in them to transfer a 75% controlling stake to citizens within three years, although reports differ on the details (ZimLII; Daily News, 2025; Business Times, 2026). This protects MSME space in transport and crafts but deters foreign joint-venture capital in those chains." },
  { h: 3, t: "Sector-specific rules in the priority chains" },
  { p: "Regulation also shapes which MSMEs can exist in each chain and who can invest in them. The Macro Diagnostic's findings and the later rules described above combine as follows." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Sector-specific regulatory factors affecting MSME readiness in the priority chains",
    cols: [1300, 4300, 3426],
    head: ["Country", "Factor", "Effect on readiness and investment"],
    rows: [
      ["Botswana", "Fresh-produce and general-dealer retail licences reserved for citizens; small-scale food and beverage manufacturing closed to foreign shareholding (MD p. 31). Foreign equity banned in touristic transfer companies (MD p. 32, 34). Guesthouses citizen-reserved (MD p. 34). Economic Inclusion Act 2021 promotes participation of targeted citizens", "Protects MSME space and creates demand for citizen suppliers, but closes processing, distribution and transport to outside capital; growth depends on domestic finance and lodge or DMC contracting"],
      ["Zambia", "Road service licences require at least 25% Zambian membership and all-Zambian directors unless the applicant holds a ZDA licence (MD p. 44). Licensing burden high; omnibus licensing for accommodation under discussion", "Favours local transport MSMEs; limits joint ventures with foreign partners; licensing cost falls on micro-enterprises"],
      ["Angola", "Foreign shareholding capped at 49% in road passenger transport and 50% in road freight (MD p. 26). INAPEM certification requested by banks for loans", "Requires local partners; thin local enterprise base in Cuando Cubango; certification is an extra step before finance"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "Withholding tax of 30% on payments to suppliers without tax clearance (MD p. 74; operator-reported). Reserved sectors for citizens include passenger transport and local arts and crafts; SI 215 of 2025 tightened rules on foreign participation", "Strong penalty on informality; makes tax clearance a precondition for supplying anchors; protects MSME space in transport and crafts but deters foreign JV capital"],
      ["Namibia", "Conservancy JV framework; end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025, with demand shifting (MD p. 117-118); slow company registration", "Mature community framework but low MSME value capture; demand shifts affect supplier viability; registration delays slow formalisation"],
      ["Regional", "KAZA Univisa operates for Zambia and Zimbabwe only; COMESA Simplified Trade Regime covers Zambia and Zimbabwe only; cross-border supply chains less evidenced than visitor flows (MD p. 117-118)", "Limits cross-border sourcing and multi-country itineraries for MSMEs"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**In Botswana**, fresh-produce and general-dealer retail licences are reserved for citizens, small-scale food and beverage manufacturing is closed to foreign shareholding (MD p. 31), foreign equity is banned in touristic transfer companies (MD p. 32, 34) and guesthouses are reserved for citizens (MD p. 34), while the Economic Inclusion Act 2021 promotes the participation of targeted citizens. Together these rules protect MSME space and create demand for citizen suppliers, but they close processing, distribution and transport to outside capital, so growth depends on domestic finance and lodge or DMC contracts." },
      { p: "**In Zambia**, road service licences require at least 25% Zambian membership and all-Zambian directors unless the applicant holds a ZDA licence (MD p. 44). This favours local transport MSMEs but limits joint ventures with foreign partners, and the general licensing burden falls heavily on micro-enterprises, which is why omnibus licensing for accommodation is under discussion. **In Angola**, foreign shareholding is capped at 49% in road passenger transport and 50% in road freight (MD p. 26), which requires local partners in a province where the local enterprise base is thin, and the INAPEM certificate that banks request adds a step before finance." },
      { p: "**In Zimbabwe**, an anchor operator reports a 30% withholding tax on payments to suppliers without tax clearance (MD p. 74), a strong penalty on informality that makes tax clearance a precondition for supplying anchors. Passenger transport and local arts and crafts are reserved for citizens, and SI 215 of 2025 tightened the rules on foreign participation, protecting MSME space in these chains while deterring foreign JV capital. **In Namibia**, the conservancy JV framework is mature but MSME value capture is low, the end of visa-free travel for 31 countries in April 2025 has shifted some demand (MD p. 117-118), which affects supplier viability, and slow company registration delays formalisation. **At regional level**, the KAZA Univisa and the COMESA Simplified Trade Regime both cover only Zambia and Zimbabwe, and cross-border supply chains are less evidenced than visitor flows (MD p. 117-118), which limits cross-border sourcing and multi-country itineraries for MSMEs." },
    ] },
  { h: 3, t: "Regional frameworks" },
  { p: "The KAZA Treaty and the SADC Protocol on Trade provide the framework for transfrontier tourism and trade, but MSMEs feel the regional frameworks mainly through visas and border procedures. The **KAZA Univisa** is used in Zambia and Zimbabwe and covers day trips into Botswana through Kazungula. At the KAZA heads of state summit in Livingstone in 2024, the five Partner States agreed in principle to broaden its use, and Botswana's Vice President said Botswana would fully adopt it, but no implementation date was given (TimesLIVE, 2024). Multi-country itineraries, which favour guiding, transfer and activity MSMEs along the Victoria Falls-Chobe corridor, therefore remain more costly to sell than they need to be." },
  { p: "For small-scale suppliers, the **COMESA Simplified Trade Regime** (STR) allows traders to move small consignments across borders with simplified documents. The COMESA threshold is USD 2,000 per consignment, adopted in 2014 and recommended to be maintained in a 2025 review (COMESA, 2025), but Zimbabwe has applied a lower limit because of exchange-control rules (ZIMRA). The STR applies between Zambia and Zimbabwe, both COMESA members, but not to Botswana, Namibia or Angola. Uptake has been low. A simplified regime that also covered the other KAZA states, together with awareness and border-staff practice at the Kazungula one-stop border post, could help women traders and small producers supply lodges across borders." },
  { p: "**Implications for readiness.** Regulation in KAZA is not the main reason MSMEs are not investment-ready, but it raises the cost of every step on the readiness pathway: registration takes weeks, tax clearance is costly in Zimbabwe, contracts cannot be relied on in court, and cross-border sourcing is hard. Programmes should therefore (i) help MSMEs through registration and tax clearance when a contract requires it, using one-stop centres where they work; (ii) design supplier finance around anchor-buyer payment routing rather than court enforcement; (iii) use Zambia's movable-collateral framework, Zimbabwe's collateral registry and Botswana's planned National Collateral Registry for small-supplier lending; and (iv) work with the KAZA Secretariat on Univisa adoption and a simplified regime for small cross-border suppliers." },

  { h: 2, t: "Market Access and Linkages with Larger Operators" },
  { p: "The Macro Diagnostic's findings on market access are clear. Demand exists: lodges want to buy locally, and the constraint is supplier reliability and readiness (MD p. 70-71, 128, 132). Entry can be quick once a supplier is ready; in Zimbabwe, new suppliers typically need only two or three successful bookings before being listed regularly (MD p. 75). Buyers set the bar: reliability matters more than price, new suppliers must match the largest incumbent supplier on price and quality, and support is best directed at enterprises already showing momentum (MD p. 68). Marketing, operator confidence and trade-show costs are obstacles for new suppliers (MD p. 75), and limited airline competition and high fares constrain the Victoria Falls corridor (MD p. 84, 117). Evidence from outside the diagnostic supports these findings and adds detail on the size of the market, what anchor procurement programmes have achieved, and what happens when linkages are weak." },
  { h: 3, t: "The size and direction of tourism demand" },
  { p: "Visitor demand has largely recovered from the pandemic, but it is volatile and policy-sensitive. Namibia received 1,345,168 international visitors in 2025, 6.9% fewer than the 1,444,174 recorded in 2024, while tourist arrivals alone fell by 3.2% (The Namibian, 2026). European tourist arrivals fell by 21%, a drop the tourism ministry linked both to the April 2025 change in visa policy and to safety concerns (The Namibian, 2026). Visitors from non-African countries now pay N$1,600 for a visa on arrival, which the tourism federation FENATA describes as one of the highest fees in the region, and operators reported delays and inconsistent implementation in the first months (Tourism Update, 2025). In Zimbabwe, international arrivals rose by 27% to 751,483 in the first half of 2024, and tourism receipts, including domestic spending, reached an estimated USD 493 million in that half-year (Equity Axis, 2025, citing UN Tourism). Botswana recorded between 391,264 and 421,744 international tourist arrivals a quarter in the second to fourth quarters of 2024, about three-quarters of them from SADC countries (Statistics Botswana, 2026). For MSMEs, the lesson is that demand is large enough to support local supply chains but can shift between neighbouring destinations quickly, as the Macro Diagnostic observes for Namibia and Kasane/Chobe (MD p. 117-118). Suppliers with several buyers, or buyers in more than one destination, are more resilient." },
  { h: 3, t: "What anchor procurement programmes show" },
  { p: "**Wilderness in Botswana** is the best-documented case of anchor-led local procurement in KAZA. It reports buying about BWP 292 million a year from Botswana-registered businesses, more than 80% of its procurement by value (Daily News, 2025), and its 2026 impact report puts the share at more than 86% when aviation, which must source parts internationally, is excluded (ATTA, 2026). Local procurement is not the same as MSME procurement, however. Wilderness's first Local Supplier Expo in Maun in 2024 hosted 110 local vendors and led to more than BWP 6.2 million of new contracts for citizen-owned businesses, a figure that had passed BWP 8 million by mid-2025 (Daily News, 2025), and 68 businesses have joined its supplier development programme (Mmegi, 2026). New contracts of this size are equivalent to about 3% of the company's annual local purchases, which shows both that small citizen suppliers can be brought into a large operator's supply chain and how much room remains. The programme is linked to finance through a 2025 memorandum with Stanbic Bank Botswana, under which Stanbic lends to SMMEs holding Wilderness supply contracts without traditional collateral and is repaid from contract payments that the suppliers cede to the bank (Mmegi, 2025), and to BDS through a 2026 memorandum with LEA that launched a Citizen Economic Empowerment Programme (Mmegi, 2026)." },
  { p: "**Gondwana Collection in Namibia** supports Namibian-owned suppliers and wholesalers and diversifies its supplier base by supporting small enterprises near its lodges (Gondwana Collection). At its Etosha King Nehale lodge, more than 80% of the items in the curio shop are locally crafted (Gondwana Collection). This is a useful counterpoint to the finding that about 80% of lodge décor in the Kavango and Zambezi regions is imported (MD p. 70): where an operator builds local sourcing into design and procurement from the start, local suppliers can meet lodge specifications." },
  { p: "**Hotels in Livingstone** have brought smallholder farmers into their supply chains. The Royal Livingstone hotel runs a fortnightly smallholder farmers' market at which it buys crops directly from local smallholders, who previously relied on roadside sales and middlemen, and it helped save the Maramba Women's Mushroom Farm, whose members now sell most of their produce to local hotels and resorts under long-term contracts (Anantara, operator-reported). A University of Pretoria doctoral study of tourism-agriculture linkages in Livingstone found that local procurement is the dominant channel and includes smallholders, but that supply, demand and marketing constraints limit how deeply smallholders are integrated (Nsanzya, University of Pretoria). These findings mirror the Macro Diagnostic's evidence from Hwange (MD p. 75)." },
  { h: 3, t: "The cost of weak linkages" },
  { p: "Earlier research shows what is at stake. A study of the Okavango Delta found that foreign ownership of tourism facilities led to revenue being repatriated, that expatriates dominated management, and that weak links with the domestic economy, particularly agriculture, limited tourism's contribution to rural development (Mbaiwa, 2005). The problem persists: Botswana's tourism ministry has said it is reviewing the Tourism Act to require tourists to pay in full in Botswana, in local currency, because of revenue repatriation, and is finalising a Tourism Leakages Study (Daily News). ODI's value-chain studies of tourism destinations found that the share of destination revenue reaching poor households ranged from about 5% to more than 25% depending on context (Ashley and Mitchell, 2007). The priority chains identified in this assessment are the main levers for raising that share in KAZA." },
  { h: 3, t: "Implications for linkage programmes" },
  { b: [
    "**Work through procurement teams, not only enterprises.** The Wilderness and Gondwana cases show that local sourcing rises when anchors set targets, specify products in advance and nominate suppliers for development.",
    "**Use anchor programmes as the gateway to finance.** Contract-backed finance (Wilderness-Stanbic) and supplier-development BDS (Wilderness-LEA) can be replicated with other operators and banks in Botswana, Zambia and Zimbabwe.",
    "**Track MSME share as well as local share.** Destination baselines (MD p. 140-141) should record how much of local procurement goes to MSMEs, women- and youth-led enterprises and community enterprises, not only to local companies.",
    "**Build multi-buyer and cross-border supply.** Aggregators that can supply several lodges, or lodges on both sides of a border, reduce the risk that demand shifts between destinations.",
    "**Address payment terms.** Micro-suppliers often finance goods for weeks between delivery and payment; faster payment by the anchor, or receivables finance, should be part of any supplier-development agreement.",
  ] },

  { h: 2, t: "Business Development Services" },
  { p: "BDS is available in all five states but is generic, concentrated in capitals and weakly connected to finance (Section 6.2). The Macro Diagnostic recommends linking supplier development to real contracts: where a supplier has a credible buyer but lacks capacity, certification, systems or working capital, the programme should close that specific constraint (MD p. 147). This section profiles the main BSOs in each country, their current programmes and how far they reach into KAZA." },
  { h: 3, t: "Angola" },
  { p: "**INAPEM** (Instituto Nacional de Apoio às Micro, Pequenas e Médias Empresas) is the lead public BSO. It certifies MSMEs, which gives access to tax benefits and is requested by banks for loan applications; in Benguela province alone it certified 3,595 enterprises in 2025, of which 3,137 were micro, 321 small and 137 medium (AllAfrica, 2026). It also provides training and business-plan support. The EU-funded Envolver project (2019-2024) qualified 2,384 MSMEs and trained 574 financial-institution staff and 376 mentors, and its successor, Envolver+, an EU twinning project led by Portugal's IAPMEI with Spain and Germany, is helping INAPEM to create and manage a national network of certified incubators between September 2025 and June 2028 with a budget of EUR 2.5 million (Ver Angola, 2025; FIIAPP). INAPEM's first decentralised incubation programme, Tuendi, started in Huíla province (Forbes África Lusófona, 2025). Other BSOs include the one-stop **Balcão Único do Empreendedor** and Guiché Único da Empresa for registration, **AIPEX** for investment facilitation, the **Câmara de Comércio e Indústria de Angola**, and Luanda-based hospitality and travel associations. In Cuando Cubango, the provincial government, **MINTUR**, the conservation area authority and NGOs such as The HALO Trust and the National Geographic Okavango Wilderness Project are more present than enterprise BSOs (Financial Landscape Analysis). No tourism-specific BDS was identified in the KAZA area." },
  { h: 3, t: "Botswana" },
  { p: "The **Local Enterprise Authority (LEA)** is the lead BSO for citizen MSMEs, providing business development, mentoring and supplier-readiness support, including record-keeping support to tourism enterprises (Financial Landscape Analysis). Its 2026 memorandum of understanding with Wilderness, which launched a Citizen Economic Empowerment Programme to bring more citizen-owned businesses into the company's supply chain, is the most direct tourism supplier-development partnership in KAZA (Mmegi, 2026). **CEDA** provides citizen enterprise finance with post-loan mentoring. The **Botswana Tourism Organisation (BTO)** supports grading, product development and citizen tourism enterprise. **HATAB** represents the hospitality industry and has a partnership with Stanbic Bank Botswana to advance tourism, **WIBA** supports women entrepreneurs, and the **Botswana Guides Association** and **BOCOBONET** represent guides and community trusts (Financial Landscape Analysis). Private anchor programmes, notably Wilderness's supplier development programme and Local Supplier Expo, now act as BSOs in their own right (Section 7.2)." },
  { h: 3, t: "Namibia" },
  { p: "The **Namibia Investment Promotion and Development Board (NIPDB)** is the national coordinator of MSME development across sectors (NIPDB). Its K2G High Potential Pool supports export-ready micro and small businesses with at least 51% Namibian ownership for 36 months; NIPDB does not lend or give grants itself, but partner banks pledged N$300,000 to the pool in 2023 (NIPDB). With the National Planning Commission and GIZ, NIPDB also runs an N$18 million SME fund financed by the German development ministry, which offers grants of N$50,000 to N$100,000 with pitch coaching and business advice and gives priority to youth-owned (aged 18 to 34) and women-owned businesses, social enterprises and cooperatives (The Namibian). **BIPA** handles registration (Section 7.1). The **Namibia Chamber of Commerce and Industry** has branches in the Zambezi and Kavango regions, and the **Namibia Tourism Board**, **FENATA**, **HAN** and the Tour and Safari Association represent and register tourism businesses. For community enterprise, **NACSO**, **IRDNC** and the **Namibia Nature Foundation** have long supported conservancies in the Zambezi and Kavango regions, and the **Development Bank of Namibia** combines tourism finance with advisory support (Financial Landscape Analysis). NIPDB's programmes are national and not tourism-specific, so a KAZA supplier-development service would need to be built with the conservancy support organisations and lodge operators." },
  { h: 3, t: "Zambia" },
  { p: "The **Zambia Development Agency (ZDA)** promotes MSME development and investment, and the **Citizens Economic Empowerment Commission (CEEC)** provides concessional finance. The two have signed an agreement to provide SMEs with both financial and non-financial business development services, enrol high-potential businesses in entrepreneurship programmes and link funded SMEs and cooperatives to supermarkets and regional markets such as COMESA (Zambia Monitor). The **Ministry of Small and Medium Enterprise Development** sets MSME policy, the **BRRA** leads licensing reform, and the **Zambia Chamber of Small and Medium Business Associations** represents MSMEs. In tourism, the **Tourism Council of Zambia** and the **Livingstone Tourism Association** represent operators, and the **Hotel and Tourism Training Institute** and Livingstone's tourism university provide skills (Financial Landscape Analysis). Youth-focused BDS is emerging: in June 2025, UNDP and the Ministry of Youth, Sport and Arts launched a programme, implemented by BongoHive, to support 200 youth-led MSMEs in Livingstone, Kitwe and Lusaka, with grants for the five best performers in a regional pitch competition (UNDP Zambia, 2025). **Peace Parks Foundation** supports community enterprise around Simalaha and Sioma Ngwezi." },
  { h: 3, t: "Zimbabwe" },
  { p: "The **Small and Medium Enterprises Development Corporation (SMEDCO)**, a state development finance institution, combines lending with capacity building for MSMEs and cooperatives. It is rolling out a USD 15 million programme to build commercial and industrial facilities for small businesses in key economic centres (Business Times), and it has signed a memorandum of understanding with the Zimbabwe Stock Exchange to help SMEs prepare for the new Zimbabwe Entrepreneurship Exchange (ZEEX), including support for formalisation, governance and investment readiness (Newsday, 2026). **ZIDA**'s one-stop centre handles investor registration, tax and licensing services (Section 7.1). **ZimTrade** supports market linkages and exports, and the **Zimbabwe Tourism Authority** registers and grades tourism businesses. The **Tourism Business Council of Zimbabwe**, the Hospitality Association of Zimbabwe and guiding and safari associations represent operators, and the **CAMPFIRE Association** represents communities in CAMPFIRE districts such as Hwange and Binga. Women- and youth-focused finance comes from the Zimbabwe Women's Microfinance Bank and EmpowerBank (Financial Landscape Analysis)." },
  { h: 3, t: "Regional and cross-cutting BSOs" },
  { p: "The **KAZA Secretariat** in Kasane coordinates transfrontier tourism, including the Univisa. Conservation NGOs (Peace Parks Foundation, WWF, Conservation International, the African Wildlife Foundation and national partners) support community enterprise, and training institutions in Maun, Katima Mulilo, Livingstone and Hwange could host incubation. Tourism associations exist in every state but rarely provide supplier development." },
  { tbl: {
    caption: "Main BSOs by country: services, KAZA reach and position on the readiness pathway",
    cols: [1100, 2500, 2300, 1300, 1826],
    head: ["Country", "Main BSOs", "Main services", "Presence in KAZA hubs", "Tiers served"],
    rows: [
      ["Angola", "INAPEM; BUE; AIPEX; CCIA; MINTUR", "Certification, registration, training, incubator network (under development)", "Low (no tourism BDS identified)", "0-1"],
      ["Botswana", "LEA; CEDA; BTO; HATAB; WIBA; Wilderness supplier programme", "BDS, mentoring, supplier readiness, citizen finance, product development", "Medium (Maun, Kasane)", "1-2"],
      ["Namibia", "NIPDB; BIPA; NCCI; NTB; FENATA/HAN; NACSO, IRDNC, NNF; DBN", "MSME coordination, High Potential Pool, SME fund, conservancy enterprise support", "Medium for conservancies; low for MSME BDS", "0-2"],
      ["Zambia", "ZDA; CEEC; BRRA; ZCSMBA; TCZ; Livingstone Tourism Association; UNDP youth programme", "BDS with finance linkage, licensing reform, youth MSME training", "Medium (Livingstone)", "1-2"],
      ["Zimbabwe", "SMEDCO; ZIDA; ZimTrade; ZTA; TBCZ; CAMPFIRE Association", "Lending and capacity building, MSME infrastructure, one-stop registration, export linkages", "Low-Medium (Victoria Falls, Hwange)", "1-2"],
    ] },
    narr: [
      { p: "**Comparing the five countries,** Angola's main BSOs (INAPEM, the BUE, AIPEX, the chamber of commerce and MINTUR) provide certification, registration, training and an incubator network still under development; their presence in the KAZA area is low and they mainly serve Tier 0 and Tier 1 enterprises. Botswana's BSOs (LEA, CEDA, BTO, HATAB, WIBA and the Wilderness supplier programme) offer BDS, mentoring, supplier readiness, citizen finance and product development, have a medium presence in Maun and Kasane, and serve Tiers 1 and 2. Namibia's BSOs (NIPDB, BIPA, the chamber of commerce, the Namibia Tourism Board, FENATA and HAN, the conservancy support organisations and DBN) cover MSME coordination, the High Potential Pool, the SME fund and conservancy enterprise support; their presence is medium for conservancies but low for MSME BDS, and they serve Tiers 0 to 2." },
      { p: "Zambia's BSOs (ZDA, CEEC, the BRRA, the SME chamber, the Tourism Council, the Livingstone Tourism Association and the UNDP youth programme) provide BDS linked to finance, licensing reform and youth MSME training, have a medium presence in Livingstone and serve Tiers 1 and 2. Zimbabwe's BSOs (SMEDCO, ZIDA, ZimTrade, the Zimbabwe Tourism Authority, the Tourism Business Council and the CAMPFIRE Association) offer lending and capacity building, MSME infrastructure, one-stop registration and export linkages; their presence in Victoria Falls and Hwange is low to medium and they serve Tiers 1 and 2." },
    ] },
  { p: "**Assessment.** The main BSOs serve Tiers 0 to 2 with generic services. None offers a tourism supplier-development service at scale in the KAZA hubs, and only the Wilderness-LEA partnership in Botswana links BDS to a specific anchor's procurement. The most promising entry points are programmes that already combine BDS with finance or market access: the ZDA-CEEC agreement, SMEDCO's programmes and its partnership with the stock exchange, NIPDB's High Potential Pool and SME fund, INAPEM's incubator network and the anchor supplier programmes. Strengthening these, rather than creating new BSOs, is the approach recommended in Section 10.5." },

  { h: 2, t: "Policy Conditions" },
  { b: [
    "Citizen-empowerment policies in Botswana and Zambia provide concessional finance (CEDA, YDF, CEEC) that suits Tier 1 and 2 MSMEs if adapted to seasonality. In Botswana, the diamond-revenue slump has constrained public financiers' liquidity (Financial Landscape Analysis).",
    "Credit guarantee schemes operate in Namibia, Zambia, Zimbabwe and Angola; Botswana has no operational national scheme (Financial Landscape Analysis).",
    "Currency instability in Zimbabwe, Zambia and Angola complicates lending to MSMEs whose revenue may be in foreign currency while costs are local, or the reverse.",
    "Local-ownership policies are tightening in Zimbabwe (SI 215 of 2025) and remain strong in Botswana. They protect MSME space but require finance to come from domestic sources or from structures that respect ownership limits.",
    "The Macro Diagnostic recommends that pilot destinations establish baselines on local procurement, supplier contract value and locally sourced products (MD p. 140-141).",
  ] },
];
